-- allium: 3
-- pam.allium

-- Scope: Privileged Access Management (PAM) v0 — credential leasing for the
-- web vault. Distilled from bitwarden_license/bit-web/src/app/pam and
-- apps/web/src/app/pam.
--
-- Includes:
--   - Access rules (conditions attached to a collection)
--   - Access requests (member-submitted requests to open a gated cipher)
--   - Approval as a single-use grant: an approved request is activated by the
--     requester, at a time of their choosing, to start the lease
--   - AccessLeases (time-bounded access grants, minted on activation)
--   - Approver inbox / decisioning
--   - AccessLease lifecycle (active, expired, revoked, canceled) and extension
--   - Notifications: which actions mail whom, and the access push
--   - Vault export: the whole-vault export bypasses leasing; a member's export of
--     their managed collections leaves gated items out
--   - Write gating: changing a gated item needs a live lease
--   - PLANNED, not built (see the PLANNED section): the org-wide kill switch and
--     leasing freeze, and the governance dashboard
--
-- The audit trail those actions write is specified in pam-audit.allium, next to
-- this file.
--
-- Excludes:
--   - Vault encryption / cipher decryption (handled by the existing vault stack)
--   - The vault-export stack itself (scope resolution, file generation,
--     encryption) — the spec models only PAM's non-interference with it
--   - Authentication / session management (auth stack)
--   - Email templates and delivery: the spec models which action mails whom, not
--     the template
--   - Push notification transport: the spec models the event, not the wire format
--   - The PAM feature flag (FeatureFlagKeys.Pam, `pm-37044-pam-v-0`), because it is a
--     temporary rollout gate rather than domain. While it is off, every rule is inert and
--     a gated item opens to its collection members as it would without PAM (see the
--     @guidance on CipherOpenWithActiveLease). That fails OPEN, unlike the PAM licence,
--     which fails closed; if the flag outlives the rollout, this line stops being true.
--   - Clients other than the web vault. The server sends them no partial items, so a
--     gated item is invisible there (see GatedCipherView); multi-client support is PAM-121.
--
-- The request form shapes itself from AccessRequestsClient::pre_check (see
-- RequestAccessModal); there is no separate forecast read.

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- External entities (other systems own these; PAM only references them)
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

external entity Organization {
    name: String
}

external entity User {
    name: String?
    email: String
}

external entity Cipher {
    -- The vault secret being gated. PAM never touches the encrypted payload;
    -- it only references the cipher's identity and which collections it lives in.
    collections: Set<Collection>
    -- The owning organization. A gated cipher is always organization-owned, since
    -- only collections can carry a rule.
    organization: Organization?
}

external entity Collection {
    organization: Organization
    name: String
    -- The access rule that gates PAM leasing for this collection, or null when leasing
    -- is disabled for it. The rule↔collection link lives HERE, on the collection, as a
    -- single reference — which is what structurally limits a collection to at most one
    -- governing rule (there is no set to hold a second). Deleting a rule clears this
    -- link (a hard delete), leaving the collection ungoverned until it is reassigned to
    -- a new rule.
    access_rule: AccessRule?
}

-- A user's membership in a collection. Gating of the collection's contents
-- is driven entirely by the presence of an enabled AccessRule attached to
-- the collection; membership itself carries no per-member gating flag.
external entity CollectionMembership {
    member: User
    collection: Collection
}

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Gated cipher response (the partial cipher returned while access is withheld)
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- The non-sensitive projection of a Cipher the server returns in a GATED
-- response — enough for the client to render the vault row and the request /
-- banner UI without the protected secret. It carries NO sensitive fields (no
-- username, password, note, card, identity, TOTP or custom fields). Only a
-- single-item read by an ungated caller, or under a live lease, returns the full
-- cipher instead (see the cipher-open section).
--
-- Mirrors the server's PartialCipherData, which is the allowlist: every field of
-- the cipher's data not named there is dropped. Nothing is decrypted to build it;
-- the retained name and URIs stay individually encrypted. Attachments are withheld
-- entirely, metadata included, because the metadata carries each attachment's key
-- (and the download is lease-gated too).
--
-- Only the web vault is sent this shape (server PartialCipherSupport). Every other
-- client (extension, desktop, mobile, CLI, or an unrecognised device type) cannot
-- render a partial item and would blank the withheld fields if it saved one back, so
-- the server leaves gated items out of its sync and lists altogether and answers a
-- single read or a write with not-found, unless the call is one that returns the full
-- item. Such a client has no request surface either, so a gated item is invisible
-- outside the web vault. When other clients learn to render partials they should get
-- them (PAM-121).
value GatedCipherView {
    cipher: Cipher                  -- identity only (the cipher id)
    type: String                    -- the item type (login, card, note, ...)
    collections: Set<Collection>
    name: String                    -- the item's display name (encrypted)
    uris: List<String>              -- login URIs, for match / launch; empty unless type is login
    created_at: Timestamp?
    revised_at: Timestamp?
    deleted_at: Timestamp?
    archived_at: Timestamp?
    -- The caller's own flags on the item, as for any item they can reach: folder,
    -- favourite, and whether they may edit it or view its password.
    folder: String?
    favorite: Boolean
    edit: Boolean
    view_password: Boolean
}

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Named rather than inline because the two share literals (canceled, expired):
-- the checker cannot tell apart the same literal on two inline enums.
enum RequestStatus { pending | approved | denied | canceled | expired }
enum LeaseStatus { active | expired | revoked | canceled }

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Access rule (org-scoped policy attached to one or more collections)
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- A single access check evaluated when a member tries to open a gated cipher.
-- An access rule's conditions are ANDed: all must pass for an approval. A rule
-- holds at most config.max_rule_conditions of them. The same kind may appear more
-- than once and is ANDed like any other: a second human_approval changes nothing,
-- and two ip_allowlists admit only their intersection. A rule with no conditions
-- is legal and still gates: it grants automatically, on request.
value Condition {
    kind: HumanApproval | IpAllowlist
}

variant HumanApproval : Condition {
    -- The request goes to a person. The approvers are always the users who can
    -- Manage the request's collection (EligibleApprovers); the condition carries no
    -- settings. Naming specific approvers was designed and never built (see the
    -- future note by EligibleApprovers).
}

variant IpAllowlist : Condition {
    -- CIDR ranges the member's source IP must fall inside. A rule is only saved
    -- with a non-empty list of valid CIDRs (OrgAdminCreatesAccessRule). At runtime
    -- it fails closed: an empty list (reachable only through a stored row that
    -- predates that check) never matches, and neither does a request whose source IP
    -- is unknown.
    --
    -- Bounds where access STARTS, never where it is used. On a rule without human
    -- approval it is checked at submit (a miss refuses the submit) and again at every
    -- activation. On a rule with human approval it is checked only at activation, so
    -- an approver can be asked to approve a request submitted from outside the
    -- allowlist, which then fails at Start (PM-42256: the conditions inform the
    -- approver's decision rather than pre-empting it). No read re-checks it, and
    -- neither does an extension (PM-42500). Do not read this as "access to this
    -- credential only ever happens from these networks"; see the ip_allowlist open
    -- question (PAM-205).
    cidrs: List<String>
}

-- A rule carries two distinct kinds of control, evaluated at different points in
-- the lifecycle. Do not conflate them (admins may *see* them grouped as "rule
-- settings", but they are not the same axis):
--
--   - Conditions (the `conditions` list) gate the GRANT. human_approval routes the
--     request to an approver; that is structural, read from the condition's presence
--     and never from how the other conditions evaluate (PM-42256). The automated
--     conditions (ip_allowlist) are checked at submit on a rule without human
--     approval, where a failure refuses the submit, and at every activation on any
--     rule. See the rule-evaluation rules. They gate FIRST GRANTS only: an extension
--     of an existing lease is not evaluated against them (PM-42500).
--
--   - AccessLease constraints (`single_active_lease`, `max_lease_duration`, and the
--     extension settings) do NOT gate the decision — they shape the LEASE. The
--     window cap is checked at submit, the slot at lease start against state beyond
--     the request (the active-lease set for the cipher), and the extension settings
--     when the holder extends. Many members can be approved and hold approved
--     requests at once; these only limit how a lease may be formed. This is why
--     single_active_lease is a field checked in ActivateLease, not a Condition
--     variant: it answers "can a lease exist now for this cipher?", not "approve this
--     request?".
entity AccessRule {
    organization: Organization
    name: String
    description: String?
    -- Request-decision gates (see note above). ANDed: all must pass to approve.
    conditions: List<Condition>
    -- The collections this rule governs. Derived from the collections' back-reference
    -- (Collection.access_rule = this), so the single source of truth for the link is on
    -- the collection side. Because that reference is singular, at most one live rule can
    -- govern a collection; a member reaching a cipher through several collections can
    -- still surface several rules (per-path contention), which is what oldest_rule
    -- arbitrates.
    collections: Collection with access_rule = this
    -- The duration the request form pre-selects, published by the pre-check and
    -- clamped to the effective maximum; config.fallback_lease_duration when unset.
    -- It bounds nothing: the maximum below does.
    default_lease_duration: Duration?
    -- AccessLease constraint: ceiling on the lease window, checked at SUBMIT. A
    -- longer window is refused, and since the lease runs to the window's end, no
    -- lease minted under the rule runs longer than this. Null means the global
    -- ceiling (config.global_max_lease_duration), not unbounded. It does not bound
    -- the one extension a lease may take, which max_extension_duration caps, so a
    -- lease's whole life is at most this plus max_extension_duration. Checked
    -- against the rule as it was at submit; lowering it later touches no request or
    -- lease already made (see Open questions).
    max_lease_duration: Duration?
    -- AccessLease constraint: may a lease under this rule be extended at all
    -- (once), without a new approval. See MemberRequestsLeaseExtension.
    allows_extensions: Boolean
    -- AccessLease constraint: the most one extension may add. Null refuses every
    -- extension, so a rule that allows extensions must set it.
    max_extension_duration: Duration?
    -- AccessLease constraint: when true, the rule asks for a per-cipher singleton — only
    -- one AccessLease active at a time for a given cipher, across all granted users.
    -- Honored by the same union/OR logic as cipher gating: the singleton binds for a
    -- member only when EVERY collection through which they reach the cipher is
    -- governed by a single_active_lease rule. Any ungated or non-single_active_lease
    -- path is an escape that lets them activate regardless (a deliberate, admin-
    -- visible bypass, like RuleBypassableCiphers). Enforced at lease start by
    -- RuleAllowsLease against the member's paths to the cipher; contention is a
    -- manual retry (no queue), not a request-time check.
    single_active_lease: Boolean
    -- When false, the rule does not restrict access (rule is staged but inert).
    enabled: Boolean

    creation_date: Timestamp
    revision_date: Timestamp
    -- Who last created or changed the rule. Stored as the actor of the
    -- rule_created and rule_updated audit events; the audit trail, not the rules
    -- page, is where an admin sees it.
    last_edited_by: User?

    -- Projections
    has_human_approval: conditions.any(c => c.kind = HumanApproval)
    has_ip_allowlist: conditions.any(c => c.kind = IpAllowlist)
}

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Access request (member-submitted) and lease (approved grant)
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

entity AccessRequest {
    requester: User
    cipher: Cipher
    -- The member collection paired with the governing rule at submit (its
    -- access_rule is that rule). Approver eligibility keys on it: the approvers are
    -- the users who can Manage this collection (EligibleApprovers). Deciding reads
    -- nothing from the rule itself.
    collection: Collection
    -- The PIN: the rule that governed the cipher for this member at submit,
    -- resolved ONCE there (oldest wins, see oldest_rule) and stored on the request by
    -- every creation path. The pin fixes WHICH rule governs; it is not a snapshot of
    -- that rule's content. So a rule appearing or disappearing on another path never
    -- moves the request to a different rule while the pin stands, but later edits to
    -- the pinned rule itself do apply: activation re-checks its automated conditions
    -- as they are now (PM-42273, so a narrowed allowlist binds an approved request),
    -- and an extension reads its extension settings as they are now (PM-43689).
    --
    -- What happens when the pinned rule goes away:
    --   - Deleted: the delete clears every pin to it (OrgAdminDeletesAccessRule), and
    --     a cleared pin re-resolves against the member's current paths wherever it
    --     would have been read. Activation is then governed by whichever rule governs
    --     now, or activates unconditionally when none does; an extension is governed
    --     by that rule, or refused when none does (the item no longer needs a lease).
    --   - Disabled: the pin stays, and resolves to "governing nothing". Activation
    --     skips the condition check, and an extension is refused ("the rule this lease
    --     was granted under is no longer active"). Under ALL-paths gating the disabled
    --     rule's collection is an escape path, so this grants nothing the member could
    --     not already reach.
    -- Deleted and disabled differ on purpose today; whether a disabled pin should
    -- re-resolve like a deleted one is PAM-211 (see Open questions). A formal
    -- projection of "the rule a pin governs by" waits for that answer.
    rule: AccessRule?

    -- Activation window: the timestamps that bound WHEN this request may be
    -- promoted to a lease, NOT the window the requester wants access for. Pinned
    -- at submit (MemberSubmitsAccessRequest) for BOTH shapes:
    --   Scheduled  → the requester's proposed [start, end].
    --   On-demand  → [submitted_at, submitted_at + duration], where duration is
    --                rule-derived (rule.default_lease_duration ??
    --                config.fallback_lease_duration).
    -- Approval AND activation are only possible up to lease_not_after (approval
    -- may land before the window opens; activation may not). The lease minted at
    -- activation runs [now, lease_not_after]: its end is the window end, and the
    -- window's length was capped at submit (see max_lease_duration).
    --
    -- An extension carries the increment instead, [the parent's end when
    -- requested, its new end]. It is never promoted to a lease of its own; it
    -- applies in place.
    lease_not_before: Timestamp
    lease_not_after: Timestamp

    reason: String?
    submitted_at: Timestamp

    -- Resolution
    -- An approved request is a *single-use grant*: the approver has granted
    -- access, but no AccessLease exists yet. The requester activates the approved
    -- request (ActivateLease), which mints the AccessLease running to the
    -- activation-window end. Activation is NOT a status: the request stays
    -- `approved` for the rest of its life, and the lease row is the record that it
    -- started (see `activated` below). Only an approved request that never started
    -- lapses to `expired`. Single use: one approved request yields at most one lease.
    --
    -- The server stores the decision facts (the request's Action, and the lease's
    -- own Action once it ends), never a status, and derives every status from those
    -- facts and the clock when it reads (PM-42653). `expired` in particular is never
    -- written: no request sweeper exists, and the expiry rules below describe what a
    -- read reports once the window has closed.
    status: RequestStatus
    -- When a party acted: an approver decided, the system approved, the requester
    -- canceled, or an extension was decided at creation. Null when nobody acted, so a
    -- request that expired while pending has none; one that expired approved keeps
    -- its approval time.
    resolved_at: Timestamp? when status != pending
    -- The approver, or null for a system / access-rule auto-decision. Persists
    -- once set — through activation and unactivated expiry — for the audit trail.
    approver: User? when status != pending
    approver_comment: String? when status != pending
    -- The lease minted when this approved request was activated (at most one —
    -- single use). Resolved from the inverse AccessLease.request reference, so it stays
    -- correct for the request's whole life, whatever the lease's later status.
    lease: AccessLease with request = this

    -- The single upper bound on this request being actionable at all — deciding a
    -- pending request AND starting an approved one: the window end. Past it a
    -- request that is still pending, or approved and never started, reads as expired
    -- (PendingRequestExpires / ApprovedRequestExpiresUnactivated). Nothing else ages
    -- a request out: a request whose window ends a year away can wait that long. A
    -- pending decision deadline (PAM-42: 7 days from submit) is not built; if it
    -- ships, it becomes one more input to the read-time derivation, not a sweeper.
    -- MyAccessRequestsPage exposes it for the activation countdown.
    actionable_until: lease_not_after

    -- If this request is an extension of an existing lease, the parent lease.
    extension_of: AccessLease?

    -- Derived "activation": the request is approved and started. A first grant
    -- started when it produced its lease; an extension that applied counts as
    -- started from its creation. Either way it stays approved and can no longer
    -- lapse, be canceled or be retracted.
    activated: status = approved and (lease != null or extension_of != null)

    -- In flight: a first grant still pending, or approved and never started. What
    -- MemberSubmitsAccessRequest refuses to duplicate (see
    -- AtMostOneInFlightRequestPerRequesterCipher).
    in_flight: extension_of = null
               and (status = pending or (status = approved and lease = null))

    -- ActionableForRequester: the request still needs something from its requester.
    -- It is awaiting a decision, or approved, never started, and inside its window.
    -- Extensions never qualify, because they are decided and applied in the step that
    -- creates them. Drives MyAccessRequestsPage's outstanding rows and the
    -- PamNavBadge count (client isActionableRequest).
    actionable_for_requester: in_flight and (status = pending or now < lease_not_after)

    transitions status {
        pending -> approved
        pending -> denied
        pending -> canceled
        pending -> expired
        approved -> expired
        approved -> denied
        approved -> canceled
        terminal: denied, canceled, expired
    }
}

entity AccessLease {
    request: AccessRequest
    cipher: Cipher
    collection: Collection
    requester: User

    not_before: Timestamp
    not_after: Timestamp

    -- How the lease left `active`: it ran out (expired), its holder ended it early
    -- (canceled, MemberEndsOwnLease), or an operator did (revoked,
    -- ApproverRevokesLease). The holder's own end is canceled even when the holder
    -- could also manage the collection. Only a revoke notifies the holder.
    status: LeaseStatus
    -- Non-optional under their when-clause: every transition into `revoked` or
    -- `canceled` is structurally obliged to stamp both (SettleLease,
    -- OrgAdminTriggersKillSwitch).
    ended_at: Timestamp when status = revoked | canceled
    ended_by: User when status = revoked | canceled
    -- The lease holds no reason of its own. A reason given for ending it is
    -- recorded in the originating request's decision log, which is where the
    -- read models take it from; the lease's own reason fields are always empty on
    -- the wire.

    -- Projections
    -- The lease stores no rule of its own: its rule is its originating request's
    -- pin, so a rule delete leaves it none (see AccessRequest.rule).
    rule: request.rule
    is_in_window: not_before <= now and now < not_after
    has_expired: now >= not_after

    transitions status {
        active -> expired
        active -> revoked
        active -> canceled
        terminal: expired, revoked, canceled
    }
}

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- The duration pre-selected when an access rule sets no default of its own,
    -- clamped to the rule's maximum (server LeaseDurationBounds).
    fallback_lease_duration: Duration = 1.hour

    -- The most conditions one rule may hold.
    max_rule_conditions: Integer = 10

    -- The ceiling on any lease window, and on every duration a rule may hold
    -- (server LeaseDurationBounds, raised from 24 hours by server #8378). A rule with
    -- no max_lease_duration is capped here, so the rule editor's "No cap" means one
    -- year.
    global_max_lease_duration: Duration = 365.days

    -- How far back resolved requests are surfaced in history views: the
    -- approver inbox history and the member's own requests page share this
    -- one time-based window. Enforced server-side (listInboxHistory /
    -- listMyRequests).
    history_window: Duration = 90.days
}

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Cipher-open gating: deriving whether a cipher is gated for the caller
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- A member opens a cipher. Access in Bitwarden is the UNION of the member's
-- collection memberships: the member reaches the cipher through any collection
-- they belong to that contains it. Gating therefore applies only when EVERY such
-- path is gated — every collection through which THIS member reaches the cipher
-- carries an enabled AccessRule. If even one of those collections has no rule,
-- the member already has ungated access, so the cipher opens normally and the
-- rule on the other collection cannot gate it. This is a real bypass, and it is
-- currently SILENT to admins (see RuleBypassableCiphers).
--
-- When several rules gate the item for a member (one per gated path), the OLDEST
-- rule takes precedence: the enabled rule with the earliest creation_date, ties
-- broken on rule id. Its conditions alone decide; a younger rule on another path
-- adds nothing. See oldest_rule.
--
-- The SERVER decides; the client only renders. The member's vault holds a gated
-- cipher as a GatedCipherView, from sync. The secret is released only by a
-- single-item read, which returns the full cipher when the caller is ungated or
-- holds a live lease. Sync, list and export reads, and the item a write echoes back,
-- always carry the partial shape, lease or not, so the secret never lands in a
-- client's local store where it would outlive the lease. The client learns that an
-- item is gated from the server's partial flag (CipherView.partial) rather than
-- recomputing the collection paths, and reads the caller's request and lease state
-- separately (AccessRequestsClient::cipher_access_state). It cannot release a
-- payload the server withholds, so a tampered or stale client cannot bypass a rule.
-- The outcomes below are the server's decision for that single-item read.
--
-- Only CipherOpened carries the full cipher, and it has exactly three sources: an
-- ungated path (CipherOpenPassthrough), a single read under a live lease held by a
-- licensed member (CipherOpenWithActiveLease), and the whole-vault export
-- (VaultExportBypassesLeasing). Every other outcome carries a GatedCipherView.
--
-- Licensing comes before everything else on a gated item. A member without a PAM
-- seat (HoldsPamSeat) gets CipherOpenBlockedUnlicensed whatever they hold: a lease
-- they already have stays active in every read but releases nothing, and they can
-- neither request, start nor extend (PM-39423). Giving access back stays open to
-- them: withdrawing a request and ending a lease need no seat.
--
-- All of this holds only while the PAM feature flag is on for the deployment (see the
-- @guidance on CipherOpenWithActiveLease).
--
-- Opening a cipher is not the only way its secret reaches a member. The whole-vault
-- EXPORT releases gated ciphers in full with no lease at all, gated instead by an
-- org-wide permission that overrides leasing outright. Everything in this section
-- describes the OPEN path only; see the vault-export section.

rule CipherOpenWithActiveLease {
    when: OpenCipher(caller, target_cipher)
    let member_memberships = CollectionMembership where member = caller
                                                    and collection in target_cipher.collections
    let has_ungated_path = member_memberships.any(mm =>
        (AccessRule where enabled
                       and mm.collection in collections).count = 0)
    requires: member_memberships.count > 0
    requires: not has_ungated_path
    -- An active lease is not enough on its own: the holder must still hold a PAM
    -- seat. A de-licensed holder's lease stays active but releases nothing (see
    -- CipherOpenBlockedUnlicensed).
    requires: HoldsPamSeat(caller, target_cipher.organization)
    let active_leases = AccessLease where requester = caller
                                 and cipher = target_cipher
                                 and status = active
                                 and now < not_after
    requires: active_leases.count > 0
    -- Singleton by AtMostOneActiveLeasePerRequesterCipher, so .first is deterministic.
    ensures: CipherOpened(caller, target_cipher, active_leases.first)
    @guidance
        -- The server enforces "only CipherOpened carries the full cipher"
        -- structurally. A full cipher response cannot be built without a
        -- FullCipherAccess witness, which only the leasing gate (CipherLeaseGate)
        -- mints, and the check runs per item, so a bulk list or export fails
        -- closed. To look for a leak, look at who mints that witness. An
        -- administrative reader is leasing-gated like any member; the whole-vault
        -- export is the only context in which leasing is waived.
        --
        -- Feature flag: every CipherOpen* rule is enforced only while the PAM
        -- feature flag (FeatureFlagKeys.Pam) is on for the deployment. While it is
        -- off, the server treats every rule as inert (UnrestrictedCipherLeaseGate),
        -- a gated item opens to its collection members as it would without PAM, and
        -- no PAM record is written for those reads. Nobody gains more than their
        -- collection membership already grants; only the time-bounding layer goes.
}

rule CipherOpenBlockedUnlicensed {
    -- Gated, and the member holds no PAM seat. Comes first and replaces every other
    -- gated outcome, an active lease included: the server stops releasing a gated
    -- cipher to an unlicensed holder whatever lease they hold (LeaseCanRelease), so
    -- the banner explains the block rather than narrating access that is no longer
    -- served. Request access is withheld too, since the submit would be refused.
    when: OpenCipher(caller, target_cipher)
    let member_memberships = CollectionMembership where member = caller
                                                    and collection in target_cipher.collections
    let has_ungated_path = member_memberships.any(mm =>
        (AccessRule where enabled
                       and mm.collection in collections).count = 0)
    requires: member_memberships.count > 0
    requires: not has_ungated_path
    requires: not HoldsPamSeat(caller, target_cipher.organization)
    ensures: CipherOpenBlockedUnlicensed(caller, target_cipher,
                                         PartialView(caller, target_cipher))
}

rule CipherOpenRequiresAccessRequest {
    -- Gated (all of the member's paths to the cipher carry a rule), no active
    -- lease and no approved request → the member must request access. No
    -- AccessRequest is created here; the open only signals that the Request
    -- Access modal should be shown (created on MemberSubmitsAccessRequest).
    when: OpenCipher(caller, target_cipher)
    let member_memberships = CollectionMembership where member = caller
                                                    and collection in target_cipher.collections
    let has_ungated_path = member_memberships.any(mm =>
        (AccessRule where enabled
                       and mm.collection in collections).count = 0)
    requires: member_memberships.count > 0
    requires: not has_ungated_path
    requires: HoldsPamSeat(caller, target_cipher.organization)
    -- The rule governing the member's access: the oldest (earliest creation_date)
    -- enabled rule among the member's gated paths to the cipher. A
    -- deterministic choice, pinned on the AccessRequest at submit. See oldest_rule.
    let member_collections = member_memberships -> collection
    let governing_candidates = AccessRule where enabled
                                            and collections.any(c => c in member_collections)
    -- oldest_rule breaks creation_date ties stably on rule id; the ordering itself
    -- is asserted structurally below so creation_date drives resolution.
    let gating_rule = oldest_rule(governing_candidates)
    requires: gating_rule != null
    requires: governing_candidates.all(r => gating_rule.creation_date <= r.creation_date)
    let active_leases = AccessLease where requester = caller
                                 and cipher = target_cipher
                                 and status = active
                                 and now < not_after
    requires: active_leases.count = 0
    -- CipherOpenWithApprovedRequest handles the approved-and-not-started case.
    let approved_requests = AccessRequest where requester = caller
                                             and cipher = target_cipher
                                             and status = approved
                                             and lease = null
                                             and extension_of = null
    requires: approved_requests.count = 0
    ensures: CipherOpenNeedsRequest(caller, target_cipher, gating_rule,
                                    PartialView(caller, target_cipher))
    @guidance
        -- Implementation: the item is already in the vault as a partial cipher from
        -- sync, and the client reads CipherView.partial to know it is gated. The
        -- banner then reads the caller's own state for that cipher through
        -- AccessRequestsClient::cipher_access_state, and offers Request access when
        -- no lease, approved request, or pending request comes back. Submitting calls
        -- AccessRequestsClient::request, whose result may already be approved when
        -- the rule needs no human approval. Enforced only while the PAM feature flag
        -- is on (see CipherOpenWithActiveLease).
}

rule CipherOpenWithApprovedRequest {
    -- The member holds an approved (unactivated) request for this cipher but no
    -- active lease. Opening offers to start the lease rather than creating a
    -- duplicate request: the partial view opens and the banner offers Start
    -- access (or shows the upcoming window when a scheduled start has not
    -- arrived). Activation stays an explicit member action — see the
    -- MemberActivatesLease section.
    when: OpenCipher(caller, target_cipher)
    let member_memberships = CollectionMembership where member = caller
                                                    and collection in target_cipher.collections
    let has_ungated_path = member_memberships.any(mm =>
        (AccessRule where enabled
                       and mm.collection in collections).count = 0)
    requires: member_memberships.count > 0
    requires: not has_ungated_path
    requires: HoldsPamSeat(caller, target_cipher.organization)
    let active_leases = AccessLease where requester = caller
                                 and cipher = target_cipher
                                 and status = active
                                 and now < not_after
    requires: active_leases.count = 0
    -- Approved and never started: a request that already produced its (now ended)
    -- lease stays approved but offers nothing here.
    let approved_requests = AccessRequest where requester = caller
                                             and cipher = target_cipher
                                             and status = approved
                                             and lease = null
                                             and extension_of = null
    requires: approved_requests.count > 0
    -- Singleton by AtMostOneInFlightRequestPerRequesterCipher, so .first is
    -- deterministic.
    ensures: CipherOpenAwaitingActivation(caller, target_cipher, approved_requests.first,
                                          PartialView(caller, target_cipher))
    @guidance
        -- A failed Start (the rule's conditions refuse it, or the single active
        -- lease slot is taken) shows a toast and leaves the request approved; it
        -- never falls through to Request access (cipher-view-banner.component,
        -- activateRequest). Enforced only while the PAM feature flag is on (see
        -- CipherOpenWithActiveLease).
}

rule CipherOpenPassthrough {
    -- Opens normally: either the member is in no collection containing the
    -- cipher, or they reach it through at least one collection with no enabled
    -- rule — so gating does not apply (union-based collection access; the bypass
    -- is not surfaced to admins — see RuleBypassableCiphers).
    when: OpenCipher(caller, target_cipher)
    let member_memberships = CollectionMembership where member = caller
                                                    and collection in target_cipher.collections
    let has_ungated_path = member_memberships.any(mm =>
        (AccessRule where enabled
                       and mm.collection in collections).count = 0)
    requires: member_memberships.count = 0 or has_ungated_path
    ensures: CipherOpened(caller, target_cipher, null)
    @guidance
        -- While the PAM feature flag is off, every open takes this path, whatever
        -- the rules say (see CipherOpenWithActiveLease).
}

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Write gating: changing a gated cipher needs a live lease
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

rule MemberChangesGatedCiphers {
    -- A member edits, deletes, restores, moves or re-files items. Any item gated for
    -- them needs a live lease on it. A bulk change refuses the WHOLE batch when any
    -- one item fails, and the refusal reads as not-found, the answer an item the
    -- caller cannot see would get (server 720cef135). A lease is enough because a
    -- write copies no secret out, and the item the write echoes back is still the
    -- partial shape. One write this lets through does matter: re-filing under a
    -- lease, see the named bypass next to RuleBypassableCiphers.
    when: MemberChangesCiphers(caller, targets)
    requires: targets.all(t =>
        not GatedFor(caller, t)
        or (AccessLease where requester = caller
                           and cipher = t
                           and status = active
                           and now < not_after).count > 0)
    ensures: CipherChangesAccepted(caller, targets)
    @guidance
        -- Enforced in CipherLeaseGate (EnsureCanMutateAsync for one item,
        -- EnsureCanMutateManyAsync for a batch), only while the PAM feature flag is
        -- on.
}

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Vault export: two paths, one bypass
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Leasing gates the cipher-OPEN path (the sections above). Export takes one of two
-- paths, and PAM treats them in opposite ways (PM-42352):
--
--   - The WHOLE-VAULT export (Owner, Admin, or Custom with Import/export) bypasses
--     leasing. It releases every cipher in its scope in full, with no AccessRequest,
--     no AccessLease and no approver, even where every path to a cipher carries an
--     enabled rule (VaultExportBypassesLeasing).
--
--   - A member's export of the collections they MANAGE leaves out every cipher gated
--     for them, whether or not they hold a lease on it, and the export screen says
--     how many were left out before the download
--     (ManagedCollectionsExportExcludesGated).
--
-- Why a lease does not unlock export: export is a bulk read, like sync. A lease
-- expires and an exported file does not, so honouring the lease would mint a copy
-- of the credential that outlives the grant (PM-42352).
--
-- The whole-vault export is a second deliberate bypass, on a different AXIS from the
-- union/OR one. RuleBypassableCiphers is a per-path escape: one ungated collection
-- undoes one rule for the members who reach the cipher that way. This is a
-- per-caller escape: one permission undoes every rule in the org, for every cipher
-- in the export, in a single action. Granting it should therefore be read as
-- granting standing, lease-free access to every gated credential in the org.
--
-- The export stack itself (scope resolution, file generation, encryption) is out of
-- scope here (see the Excludes block), so no PAM surface provides these triggers:
-- they arrive from the existing vault-export paths.

rule VaultExportBypassesLeasing {
    when: ExportOrganizationVault(caller, organization)
    requires: CanExportOrganizationVault(caller, organization)
    -- Every cipher in the export's scope is released in full with no lease
    -- authorizing it: the same outcome as an ungated open (CipherOpenPassthrough),
    -- reached by permission rather than by an ungated path.
    ensures:
        for c in ExportedCiphers(caller, organization):
            CipherOpened(caller, c, null)
}

rule ManagedCollectionsExportExcludesGated {
    -- Any confirmed member may export the collections they manage. Each cipher in
    -- that scope that is gated for them is left out, lease or no lease; the rest
    -- export as the export stack resolves them.
    when: ExportManagedCollections(caller, organization)
    ensures:
        for c in ManagedExportCiphers(caller, organization):
            if GatedFor(caller, c):
                CipherLeftOutOfExport(caller, c)
            else:
                CipherOpened(caller, c, null)
    @guidance
        -- The web vault builds this export from its local store, where a gated item
        -- is only ever a partial, and leaves partials out of every format (JSON,
        -- CSV, encrypted); it shows the left-out count before the download
        -- (PM-42531). The server's managed-collections export endpoint drops them
        -- too.
}

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Member-initiated transitions
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

rule MemberSubmitsAccessRequest {
    -- The member submits the Request Access modal for a gated cipher. THIS is
    -- where the AccessRequest is created — not at cipher-open. The activation
    -- window is pinned here for BOTH shapes: a proposed window (window_start +
    -- window_end) makes it a scheduled request; otherwise the window defaults to
    -- [now, now + rule-derived duration] — an on-demand request, promotable
    -- immediately. The requester never names a duration: the on-demand window
    -- length comes from the pinned rule, and the lease minted at activation runs
    -- to the window end either way (see ActivateLease). Evaluation runs
    -- immediately, so the submit response may already be approved.
    --
    -- KNOWN GAP: the built form's automatic path lets the member pick a duration
    -- (pre-filled with the rule's default, capped at its maximum) rather than
    -- taking the default. Whether every rule should submit the same shape, always a
    -- window, is PAM-207; this rule keeps the older shape until that is decided.
    when: MemberSubmitsRequest(caller, target_cipher, reason?, window_start?, window_end?)
    -- A proposed window is all-or-nothing: a start without an end (or vice
    -- versa) is rejected rather than half-defaulted.
    requires: window_start != null implies window_end != null
    requires: window_end != null implies window_start != null
    let memberships = CollectionMembership where collection in target_cipher.collections
                                              and member = caller
    requires: memberships.count > 0
    -- Acquiring access needs a PAM seat (PM-39423). The refusal copy says nothing
    -- about the rule, so it leaks no configuration.
    requires: HoldsPamSeat(caller, target_cipher.organization)
    -- Gated at all? The same ALL-paths test the CipherOpen* rules use: one path with
    -- no enabled rule means the item is not gated for this member, and there is
    -- nothing to request.
    let has_ungated_path = memberships.any(mm =>
        (AccessRule where enabled
                       and mm.collection in collections).count = 0)
    requires: not has_ungated_path
    -- Governing-rule resolution, computed once here and PINNED on the created
    -- AccessRequest.rule below (its RuleId stored at creation). The candidate set is
    -- the enabled rules on the collections through which the member
    -- reaches the cipher; the oldest (earliest creation_date) governs. The requires
    -- clause asserts no candidate is older, so creation_date drives the choice
    -- structurally; oldest_rule additionally breaks ties stably on rule id.
    let member_collections = memberships -> collection
    let governing_candidates = AccessRule where enabled
                                            and collections.any(c => c in member_collections)
    let gating_rule = oldest_rule(governing_candidates)
    requires: gating_rule != null
    requires: governing_candidates.all(r => gating_rule.creation_date <= r.creation_date)
    -- The member's collections the winning rule governs. The request is filed
    -- against one of them, and that collection's managers are its approvers.
    let paired_memberships = memberships where collection.access_rule = gating_rule
    -- A rule without human approval checks its automated conditions HERE, against
    -- the member's current request context. A failure refuses the submit and nothing
    -- is saved: no request, no decision, and today no audit event either (PAM-229
    -- adds one; whether to save a denied request too is PAM-206). A rule with human
    -- approval leaves them to activation.
    requires: gating_rule.has_human_approval
              or RuleAutomatedConditionsPass(gating_rule, caller)
    -- The window, and its cap. The lease minted from this request runs to the
    -- window's end, so capping the window HERE caps the lease: its span may not
    -- exceed the rule's max_lease_duration, or the global ceiling when the rule sets
    -- none, and never the ceiling itself (PM-39858, server #8378). Too long a window
    -- is refused, not trimmed.
    let effective_max =
        if gating_rule.max_lease_duration != null
           and gating_rule.max_lease_duration < config.global_max_lease_duration:
            gating_rule.max_lease_duration
        else: config.global_max_lease_duration
    -- The on-demand length is the pre-fill: the rule's default, or the fallback,
    -- clamped to the effective maximum so it can never exceed what is accepted.
    let requested_default = gating_rule.default_lease_duration ?? config.fallback_lease_duration
    let effective_default = if requested_default > effective_max: effective_max
                            else: requested_default
    let window_open = window_start ?? now
    let window_close = window_end ?? (now + effective_default)
    requires: window_close - window_open <= effective_max
    let active_leases = AccessLease where requester = caller
                                 and cipher = target_cipher
                                 and status = active
                                 and now < not_after
    requires: active_leases.count = 0
    -- No duplicate while another request for this cipher is in flight: pending,
    -- or approved and not started. A request that started and whose lease has
    -- since ended blocks nothing.
    let in_flight_requests = AccessRequest where requester = caller
                                              and cipher = target_cipher
                                              and in_flight
    requires: in_flight_requests.count = 0
    ensures: AccessRequest.created(
        requester: caller,
        cipher: target_cipher,
        collection: paired_memberships.first.collection,
        rule: gating_rule,
        lease_not_before: window_open,
        lease_not_after: window_close,
        reason: reason,
        submitted_at: now,
        status: pending
    )
    ensures: AccessChanged(requester: caller, collection: paired_memberships.first.collection)
}

rule MemberCancelsOwnRequest {
    -- The requester withdraws their own not-yet-activated request: still pending, or
    -- approved but never started (once a lease is minted, that access is ended through
    -- the lease instead). Only inside the window: past lease_not_after the request
    -- reads as expired, and the server refuses to restamp it (PM-42653). An extension
    -- cannot be withdrawn at all; it applied in the step that created it.
    when: MemberCancelsRequest(member, request)
    requires: request.status in {pending, approved}
    requires: request.lease = null
    requires: request.extension_of = null
    requires: now < request.lease_not_after
    requires: request.requester = member
    ensures: request.status = canceled
    ensures: request.resolved_at = now
    -- No decision was made; approver fields are present (status != pending) but
    -- empty.
    ensures: request.approver = null
    ensures: request.approver_comment = null
    ensures: AccessChanged(requester: request.requester, collection: request.collection)
}

rule MemberRequestsLeaseExtension {
    -- The lease holder asks to push its end out by a duration they name, with a
    -- justification. Recorded as a fresh AccessRequest pointing back at the parent
    -- lease, but one that is never waited on: extensions never require an approval
    -- (PM-42500), so the request is created ALREADY DECIDED, in one step, and never
    -- passes through pending or evaluation. Its window is the increment, from the
    -- parent's current end to its new end.
    --
    -- Parent live: created approved (a system decision, no approver) and applied in
    -- place, pushing the parent's end out by the requested duration; no new lease is
    -- minted. Parent no longer live (it expired, or was revoked or ended): created
    -- denied with a reason, so the requester can inspect a late extension instead of
    -- getting a bare error (PM-42632). There is no liveness check before that, and
    -- the denied request still counts as the lease's one extension. The client only
    -- offers Extend on a live lease, so only the API reaches the late case.
    --
    -- Either way the request is final at creation: an applied extension stays
    -- approved and cannot be canceled or retracted (the server and client still
    -- allow it, PAM-222).
    when: MemberRequestsExtension(member, lease, duration, reason)
    requires: lease.requester = member
    -- An extension buys new access, so it needs a PAM seat like any acquisition.
    requires: HoldsPamSeat(member, lease.collection.organization)
    -- The rule read here is the lease's rule as it stands now: the pin of the
    -- request that minted it. A disabled pin refuses the extension; a pin cleared by
    -- a delete re-resolves against the member's current paths first, and refuses
    -- when no rule governs (see AccessRequest.rule).
    let governing = lease.rule
    requires: governing.enabled
    requires: governing.allows_extensions
    requires: duration > 0.seconds
    -- The rule's per-increment cap; a rule with no cap set refuses.
    requires: duration <= governing.max_extension_duration
    requires: reason != null and trim(reason) != ""
    -- One extension per lease (PM-40526), whatever became of an earlier attempt:
    -- a denied late extension counts too. See AtMostOneExtensionPerLease.
    requires: (AccessRequest where extension_of = lease).count = 0
    let parent_live = lease.status = active and now < lease.not_after
    let old_end = lease.not_after
    let new_end = lease.not_after + duration
    ensures:
        if parent_live:
            AccessRequest.created(
                requester: lease.requester,
                cipher: lease.cipher,
                collection: lease.collection,
                rule: governing,
                lease_not_before: old_end,
                lease_not_after: new_end,
                reason: reason,
                submitted_at: now,
                status: approved,
                resolved_at: now,
                approver: null,
                approver_comment: null,
                extension_of: lease
            )
            lease.not_after = new_end
        else:
            AccessRequest.created(
                requester: lease.requester,
                cipher: lease.cipher,
                collection: lease.collection,
                rule: governing,
                lease_not_before: old_end,
                lease_not_after: new_end,
                reason: reason,
                submitted_at: now,
                status: denied,
                resolved_at: now,
                approver: null,
                approver_comment: "The lease being extended has ended",
                extension_of: lease
            )
    ensures: AccessChanged(requester: lease.requester, collection: lease.collection)
}

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rule evaluation: deciding a pending request
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- A request is created and becomes ready for evaluation in one step when the
-- member submits the Request Access modal (MemberSubmitsAccessRequest). An
-- extension never comes here: it is created already decided. Every gated open —
-- including IP-allowlist-only rules
-- — goes through the modal/submit. A rule with no human_approval condition
-- resolves synchronously on submit, so the submit response can already carry an
-- approved request. Its automated conditions were already checked by the submit
-- itself, which refuses on a failure and saves nothing, so evaluation never meets a
-- failing request: there is no automatic denial of a first grant. A rule with
-- human_approval routes to an approver whatever the automated conditions say; they
-- are checked at activation instead (PM-42256, PM-42273).
--
-- AN EXTENSION IS NOT EVALUATED AGAINST THE RULE AT ALL (PM-42500). A rule's
-- conditions gate the FIRST grant only. Extending a lease the member already holds
-- is not a fresh grant, so it is approved as it is created
-- (MemberRequestsLeaseExtension): no approver sees it, AND no automated condition
-- is asked either. An extension requested from outside the rule's ip_allowlist
-- still applies.
--
-- The reasoning, stated plainly because the consequence is not small: the decision
-- that mattered was the original grant, and an extension re-opens no new access —
-- it lengthens access the member already holds, over the same credential, under the
-- same rule. The controls that remain on it are the rule's opt-in (may this lease
-- be extended at all), the per-increment cap, and one extension per lease. What
-- this gives up is that an
-- ip_allowlist can no longer bound access to the admitted network once a lease
-- exists: a member who obtained a lease from inside the allowlist may extend it
-- from anywhere. That is a deliberate trade, not an oversight — see the
-- ip_allowlist open question (PAM-205).
--
-- The two evaluation rules below partition the pending space exactly once, so no
-- request matches both (a pending request is always a first grant):
--   no human_approval  -> AutoApproveWhenNoHumanApproval
--   human_approval     -> RouteToHumanApprovers

rule RequestReadyForEvaluationOnCreation {
    -- Requests are only ever created by a member submitting the Request Access
    -- modal (MemberSubmitsAccessRequest) or by a lease extension
    -- (MemberRequestsLeaseExtension) — never speculatively at cipher-open. Only the
    -- first is created pending; it is, by construction, one the member has
    -- submitted, and is immediately ready for evaluation.
    when: request: AccessRequest.created
    requires: request.status = pending
    ensures: RequestReadyForEvaluation(request)
}

rule AutoApproveWhenNoHumanApproval {
    -- The access rule has no human_approval condition: approve the request
    -- automatically on submit. Its automated conditions (e.g. ip_allowlist) already
    -- passed, since the submit refuses otherwise. No lease is minted here; the
    -- requester starts it on activation, where the conditions are checked again.
    when: RequestReadyForEvaluation(request)
    requires: request.status = pending
    -- First grants only, stated locally: an extension is never pending.
    requires: request.extension_of = null
    requires: not request.rule.has_human_approval
    ensures: request.status = approved
    ensures: request.resolved_at = now
    ensures: request.approver = null
    @guidance
        -- The submit (POST) returns this already-approved request, so the UI
        -- can offer "Start access now" immediately rather than showing a
        -- pending state. See ActivateLease.
}

rule RouteToHumanApprovers {
    -- The rule needs human approval: leave the request pending and signal the
    -- approver inbox. The automated conditions are NOT asked here. On a human rule
    -- they inform the approver's decision rather than pre-empting it (PM-42256), and
    -- bind at activation instead (PM-42273), so an approver can be asked about a
    -- request submitted from outside the allowlist, which then fails at Start. First
    -- grants only: an extension is never routed to a human (PM-42500), even under a
    -- human_approval rule; it is decided as it is created.
    when: RequestReadyForEvaluation(request)
    requires: request.status = pending
    requires: request.extension_of = null
    requires: request.rule.has_human_approval
    ensures: RequestAwaitingApprover(request)
    ensures: ApproversNotified(request)
    @guidance
        -- Nothing yet shows the approver the requester's network, so "inform" is
        -- aspirational today.
}

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Approver decisioning
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

rule ApproverApprovesRequest {
    -- The approver approves the request. No lease yet — the requester activates it
    -- (ActivateLease) when they actually need access.
    when: ApproverDecides(approver, request, verdict, comment?)
    requires: request.status = pending
    -- First grants only. Extensions never require an approval (PM-42500) and never
    -- rest at pending, so no approver can decide one; stated locally here rather
    -- than left to a reachability argument.
    requires: request.extension_of = null
    -- Deciding reads nothing from the pinned rule. A pending first grant only ever
    -- exists under a human_approval rule (a rule without one resolves at submit),
    -- and eligibility keys on request.collection, so a pin cleared by a rule delete
    -- in the meantime changes nothing here.
    requires: verdict = approve
    requires: approver in EligibleApprovers(request)
    -- Self-approval guard: an approver cannot approve their own request. Enforced
    -- by the server's DecideAccessRequestCommand; canApprove.ts only mirrors it in
    -- the UI.
    requires: approver != request.requester
    ensures: request.status = approved
    ensures: request.resolved_at = now
    ensures: request.approver = approver
    -- Optional on approve; a blank comment is stored as none.
    ensures: request.approver_comment = comment
    ensures: RequesterNotifiedOfDecision(request, verdict)
    ensures: AccessChanged(requester: request.requester, collection: request.collection)
}

rule ApproverDeniesRequest {
    when: ApproverDecides(approver, request, verdict, comment?)
    requires: request.status = pending
    -- First grants only, mirroring ApproverApprovesRequest: an extension is never
    -- put to an approver at all, to approve or to deny (PM-42500).
    requires: request.extension_of = null
    requires: verdict = deny
    requires: approver in EligibleApprovers(request)
    requires: approver != request.requester
    -- A deny needs a reason (PM-39666): it reaches the requester's notification,
    -- the resolved history and the audit trail, and there is no later chance to
    -- add it.
    requires: comment != null and trim(comment) != ""
    ensures: request.status = denied
    ensures: request.resolved_at = now
    ensures: request.approver = approver
    ensures: request.approver_comment = comment
    ensures: RequesterNotifiedOfDecision(request, verdict)
    ensures: AccessChanged(requester: request.requester, collection: request.collection)
}

rule ApproverRetractsRequest {
    -- A managing approver withdraws an approval that was never started. Recorded
    -- as a denial BY the approver so the audit trail names them, distinct from a
    -- member's own cancellation (MemberCancelsOwnRequest), which records no approver.
    -- No comment is captured today (PAM-29 would require a reason; that is the next
    -- change here and in the code). Only inside the window: past lease_not_after the
    -- request reads as expired, and the server refuses to restamp it (PM-42653).
    --
    -- Out of scope: a PENDING request, which the approver decides instead, with its
    -- required deny reason (PAM-29 defines retraction as approved -> denied; the
    -- server still accepts a manager's cancel of a pending request, PAM-225); a
    -- started request, whose access is ended through the lease
    -- (ApproverRevokesLease); and an extension, which applied in the step that
    -- created it (PAM-222 is the server and client bug that still allows it).
    when: ApproverCancelsRequest(approver, request)
    requires: request.status = approved
    requires: request.lease = null
    requires: request.extension_of = null
    requires: now < request.lease_not_after
    requires: approver in EligibleApprovers(request)
    requires: approver != request.requester
    ensures: request.status = denied
    ensures: request.resolved_at = now
    ensures: request.approver = approver
    ensures: request.approver_comment = null
    -- Pushed, but not mailed: the requester is not told by mail that their approval
    -- was withdrawn (see Open questions).
    ensures: AccessChanged(requester: request.requester, collection: request.collection)
}

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Activation: the requester starts the lease at a time of their choosing
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- An approved request is activated via MemberActivatesLease. Activation is ALWAYS an
-- explicit member action — a Start click on MyAccessRequestsPage, the
-- CipherViewBanner or the RequestDetailView — never a side effect of opening the
-- item, and never automatic:
-- minting the lease requires the requester to be online to drive the client (this
-- is why even a scheduled request must be started by the requester inside its
-- window, rather than springing to life on its own when the window opens).
--
-- The two request shapes differ ONLY in how their activation window was
-- populated at submit (a proposed [start, end] for scheduled; [submitted_at,
-- submitted_at + rule-derived duration] for on-demand). From here on they are
-- UNIFORM: one start rule (ActivateLease), one eligibility window
-- [lease_not_before, actionable_until], one unactivated-expiry rule
-- (ApprovedRequestExpiresUnactivated), and one lease-END formula — the lease
-- runs from the moment of activation (never backdated to the window start) to
-- the window end. The rule's durations already shaped and capped the window at
-- submit; neither plays a part at activation.
--
-- single_active_lease contention is resolved here, at start, since this is when
-- the lease comes into being: if RuleAllowsLease is false (the cipher's single slot
-- is already taken) the start is simply rejected and the approved request stays
-- activatable for a manual retry — there is no queue. A start will likewise be
-- refused while the org is under a LeasingFreeze (the kill switch's optional
-- block-new-leases), once that is built (see the PLANNED section). Automated
-- conditions (e.g. ip_allowlist) are re-checked here too: a mismatch at start
-- rejects the activation. Extensions never reach here —
-- they apply in place as they are created (MemberRequestsLeaseExtension).
--
-- A failed activation NEVER consumes the approval. The request counts as started
-- (`activated`) ONLY once the start mints its lease (ActivateLease); the status
-- stays `approved` throughout. Any rejected start — automated-condition mismatch,
-- single_active_lease contention, leasing freeze, or a lapsed window — leaves the
-- request approved with no lease, and activatable for a retry up to
-- actionable_until (the window end). This lease↔request correspondence is pinned by
-- the AtMostOneLeasePerActivatedRequest and NoLeaseWithoutActivatedRequest
-- invariants; this prose does not restate it.
--
-- Every activation attempt is audited, success or failure: ActivateLease emits
-- LeaseActivationAudited(.., outcome: succeeded) alongside minting the lease,
-- and LeaseActivationRejected emits the same event with outcome: rejected for any
-- start that cannot proceed. The record these events write, and which refusals
-- the server writes today, are specified in pam-audit.allium.

rule ActivateLease {
    when: MemberActivatesLease(member, request)
    requires: request.status = approved
    requires: request.extension_of = null
    -- Single use: a request that already produced its lease cannot start another.
    -- Asking again returns that lease while it is live and is refused once it has
    -- ended; neither is a genuine attempt, so neither is audited.
    requires: request.lease = null
    requires: request.requester = member
    -- Starting a lease is an acquisition: it needs a PAM seat (PM-39423).
    requires: HoldsPamSeat(member, request.collection.organization)
    -- Eligibility: only inside the activation window (both shapes carry one,
    -- pinned at submit).
    requires: now >= request.lease_not_before
    requires: now < request.actionable_until
    -- Automated conditions (e.g. ip_allowlist) are re-checked at start against
    -- the pinned rule as it stands now; a mismatch rejects the activation (see
    -- LeaseActivationRejected). A disabled pin governs nothing, and a pin cleared by
    -- a delete re-resolves first (see AccessRequest.rule).
    requires: RuleAutomatedConditionsPass(request.rule, member)
    requires: RuleAllowsLease(request.requester, request.cipher)
    -- Blocked while the org is under a leasing freeze (e.g. kill switch). NOT
    -- ENFORCED TODAY: the freeze is planned (PAM-49, PAM-17), no LeasingFreeze can
    -- exist yet, so this guard is vacuous. See the PLANNED section.
    requires: not exists LeasingFreeze{organization: request.collection.organization}
    -- The lease runs from ACTIVATION (never backdated to the window start,
    -- PM-42596) to the window end. The window's length was capped at submit, and
    -- activation needs now >= lease_not_before, so the lease can never run longer
    -- than the rule's max as it was at submit. Nothing re-caps it here.
    ensures: AccessLease.created(
        request: request,
        cipher: request.cipher,
        collection: request.collection,
        requester: request.requester,
        not_before: now,
        not_after: request.lease_not_after,
        status: active
    )
    ensures: LeaseActivationAudited(request: request, outcome: succeeded)
    ensures: AccessChanged(requester: request.requester, collection: request.collection)
}

rule LeaseActivationRejected {
    -- A genuine start attempt on a real approved, member-owned, non-extension
    -- request that cannot proceed right now. Every reason a start is refused is
    -- captured here so the attempt is audited even on failure: an automated
    -- condition no longer passes (e.g. ip_allowlist), single_active_lease
    -- contention (RuleAllowsLease is false), an org leasing freeze, or the
    -- request's activation window has not opened or has lapsed. The disjunction is the
    -- exact negation of ActivateLease's remaining guards, so this rule and the start
    -- rule are mutually exclusive. Nothing is consumed: the request stays `approved`
    -- and activatable for a retry within its window (see the activation-section
    -- prose).
    --
    -- A caller without a PAM seat is NOT a genuine attempt: like a caller who does
    -- not own the request, they are refused before the attempt is recorded, so the
    -- refusal is not audited (the server checks the licence ahead of the audit
    -- Attempt). Whether an admin should get a signal for it is still open on
    -- PM-39423.
    when: MemberActivatesLease(member, request)
    requires: request.status = approved
    requires: request.extension_of = null
    requires: request.lease = null
    requires: request.requester = member
    requires: HoldsPamSeat(member, request.collection.organization)
    let cannot_start =
        not RuleAutomatedConditionsPass(request.rule, member)
        or not RuleAllowsLease(request.requester, request.cipher)
        -- Planned, vacuous today; see ActivateLease.
        or exists LeasingFreeze{organization: request.collection.organization}
        or now >= request.actionable_until
        or now < request.lease_not_before
    requires: cannot_start
    -- The audit log emits a lease_activation_rejected event at this moment (via the
    -- outcome below); each refusal is written as its own audit event. The request
    -- stays approved and re-activatable; this does not transition status.
    ensures: LeaseActivationAudited(request: request, outcome: rejected)
}

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- AccessLease lifecycle: expiry, revocation, org-wide kill switch
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Expiry and an early end are the ways a lease leaves `active`, and a single
-- transition out of `active` can have only one terminal outcome. Rather than
-- have the autonomous expiry timer, the holder's own end and an operator's
-- revoke each write `status` directly — racing each other for the terminal value
-- — all three emit a LeaseSettles event and SettleLease owns the single
-- transition. Expiry takes precedence: an early end is only emitted while
-- strictly in-window (now < not_after), so once not_after has passed the only
-- signal that can arrive is the expiry one and the lease deterministically
-- settles to `expired`. The paths are mutually exclusive by construction.
rule LeaseExpires {
    when: lease: AccessLease.not_after <= now
    requires: lease.status = active
    ensures: LeaseSettles(lease: lease, ended_by: null, reason: null)
    @guidance
        -- Like request expiry, derived at read time: a lease past not_after reads
        -- as expired with nothing written. A lease-only sweep does run, once a
        -- minute, but only to journal each natural lapse once: it emits the
        -- lease_expired audit event and the access-end rotation trigger, and still
        -- writes no status.
}

-- Request expiry is DERIVED at read time: no stored transition and no request
-- sweeper (PM-42653). The server stores what the parties did and reads a request
-- whose window has closed, still pending or approved and never started, as
-- expired. The two rules below say what such a read reports and when; the write
-- guards use the same clock comparison, so the moment matches exactly.
rule PendingRequestExpires {
    -- A pending request expires once its window closes undecided (approval is not
    -- possible outside the window).
    when: request: AccessRequest.actionable_until <= now
    requires: request.status = pending
    ensures: request.status = expired
    -- Nobody acted, so it has no resolution time and no approver.
    ensures: request.resolved_at = null
    ensures: request.approver = null
    ensures: request.approver_comment = null
}

rule ApprovedRequestExpiresUnactivated {
    -- An approved request the requester never started lapses once its window
    -- closes. resolved_at and approver keep the approval record; the lease was
    -- never minted. A request that DID start stays approved for good, and an
    -- extension, which applied at creation, never lapses either.
    when: request: AccessRequest.actionable_until <= now
    requires: request.status = approved
    requires: request.lease = null
    requires: request.extension_of = null
    ensures: request.status = expired
}

rule MemberEndsOwnLease {
    -- The holder gives their access back early. Settles to canceled, not revoked.
    -- Needs no PAM seat: a de-licensed holder can still end what they hold.
    when: MemberEndsOwnLease(member, lease, reason?)
    requires: lease.status = active
    requires: now < lease.not_after
    requires: lease.requester = member
    ensures: LeaseSettles(lease: lease, ended_by: member, reason: reason)
    ensures: AccessChanged(requester: lease.requester, collection: lease.collection)
}

rule ApproverRevokesLease {
    -- An operator cuts off someone's live lease. Settles to revoked, and the holder
    -- is told. Needs no PAM seat. When the actor happens to be the holder (a manager
    -- of the collection ending their own lease), it settles to canceled all the
    -- same.
    when: ApproverRevokesLeaseAction(actor, lease, reason?)
    requires: lease.status = active
    -- Expiry takes precedence over revocation: once not_after has passed the
    -- lease is dead regardless of whether LeaseExpires has fired yet, so a
    -- revoke at or past not_after is rejected (the exact negation of the
    -- LeaseExpires trigger). Strictly in-window, this emits the settlement
    -- signal; SettleLease performs the single terminal transition. The two
    -- paths are therefore mutually exclusive and the terminal state is
    -- deterministic.
    requires: now < lease.not_after
    requires: actor in EligibleRevokers(lease)
    ensures: LeaseSettles(lease: lease, ended_by: actor, reason: reason)
    ensures: AccessChanged(requester: lease.requester, collection: lease.collection)
}

rule SettleLease {
    -- The single owner of the transition out of `active`. Driven by exactly one
    -- emitter — the autonomous LeaseExpires timer, or an in-window
    -- MemberEndsOwnLease or ApproverRevokesLease — never two, since an early end is
    -- rejected at/past not_after. No ender means the lease lapsed on its own
    -- (expired); the holder means they gave it back (canceled); anyone else means
    -- an operator cut it off (revoked). The reason, if any, goes to the originating
    -- request's decision log, not onto the lease.
    when: LeaseSettles(lease, ended_by, reason?)
    requires: lease.status = active
    ensures:
        if ended_by = null:
            lease.status = expired
        else:
            if ended_by = lease.requester:
                lease.status = canceled
            else:
                lease.status = revoked
                HolderNotifiedOfRevoke(lease)
            lease.ended_at = now
            lease.ended_by = ended_by
}

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Notifications
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Two channels, both sent by the server and both only while the PAM feature flag is
-- on. The rules above emit them; this block states the policy once.
--
-- MAIL goes to three audiences, and only these:
--   - ApproversNotified: a request needs a decision (RouteToHumanApprovers). Sent
--     to EligibleApprovers for the request minus the requester, who cannot decide
--     their own request; when nobody else can decide it, nobody is mailed.
--   - RequesterNotifiedOfDecision: an approver approved or denied the request
--     (ApproverApprovesRequest, ApproverDeniesRequest).
--   - HolderNotifiedOfRevoke: an operator revoked the holder's lease (SettleLease's
--     revoked branch). A holder ending their own lease is not mailed.
-- Content policy, for every PAM mail: the organization's name, the window, and a
-- link to the per-request view (RequestDetailView, /pam/requests/{id}). Never the
-- requester's reason, and never a cipher or collection name; the link is where the
-- reader, once signed in and unlocked, sees the rest.
--
-- Nothing is mailed for an automatic approval, a submit the conditions refused, a
-- member's own cancel, an approver's retraction, an activation, an extension, a
-- self-end, or an expiry. Three of those silences are undecided (see Open
-- questions): the retraction, the refused submit, and expiry.
--
-- PUSH (AccessChanged) carries no content. Every rule that changes a request or a
-- lease sends it, to the requester's clients and to the managers of the request's
-- collection, so open pages and the cipher-view banner re-read instead of polling.
-- Two exceptions push the requester only: an automatic approval at submit, and an
-- extension denied because the lease had ended. Expiry sends nothing: nothing happens
-- on the server when a window closes, so every reader checks the clock itself.

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Access rule administration (CRUD)
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

rule OrgAdminCreatesAccessRule {
    when: CreateAccessRule(admin, organization, name, description?, conditions,
                            collections, default_lease_duration?, max_lease_duration?,
                            allows_extensions, max_extension_duration?,
                            single_active_lease, enabled)
    -- Rule authorship is its own permission, separate from approving requests.
    requires: CanManageAccessRules(admin, organization)
    requires: name.length > 0 and name.length <= 256
    requires: conditions.count <= config.max_rule_conditions
    -- An ip_allowlist needs at least one CIDR, and every entry must parse as one.
    requires: conditions.all(c => c.kind = IpAllowlist
                                  implies (c.cidrs.count > 0
                                           and c.cidrs.all(r => valid_cidr(r))))
    -- Durations: each one set must be positive and within the global ceiling, and
    -- the pre-fill may not exceed the maximum. A rule that allows extensions must
    -- say how much one may add.
    requires: default_lease_duration = null or default_lease_duration > 0.seconds
    requires: max_lease_duration = null or max_lease_duration > 0.seconds
    requires: default_lease_duration = null or default_lease_duration <= config.global_max_lease_duration
    requires: max_lease_duration = null or max_lease_duration <= config.global_max_lease_duration
    requires: max_extension_duration = null or max_extension_duration <= config.global_max_lease_duration
    requires: default_lease_duration = null or max_lease_duration = null
              or default_lease_duration <= max_lease_duration
    requires: allows_extensions implies max_extension_duration > 0.seconds
    requires: collections.all(c => c.organization = organization)
    -- Each target collection must be free to govern: unlinked (a hard delete clears
    -- the links of the rule it removes). A collection already governed by a live rule
    -- is rejected rather than silently stolen from it.
    requires: collections.all(c => c.access_rule = null)
    ensures:
        let created = AccessRule.created(
            organization: organization,
            name: name,
            description: description,
            conditions: conditions,
            default_lease_duration: default_lease_duration,
            max_lease_duration: max_lease_duration,
            allows_extensions: allows_extensions,
            max_extension_duration: max_extension_duration,
            single_active_lease: single_active_lease,
            enabled: enabled,
            creation_date: now,
            revision_date: now,
            last_edited_by: admin
        )
        -- Establish the link on the collection side (its single source of truth).
        for c in collections:
            c.access_rule = created
}

rule OrgAdminUpdatesAccessRule {
    when: UpdateAccessRule(admin, rule, name, description?, conditions,
                            collections, default_lease_duration?, max_lease_duration?,
                            allows_extensions, max_extension_duration?,
                            single_active_lease, enabled)
    requires: CanManageAccessRules(admin, rule.organization)
    requires: name.length > 0 and name.length <= 256
    -- The same shape checks as create.
    requires: conditions.count <= config.max_rule_conditions
    requires: conditions.all(c => c.kind = IpAllowlist
                                  implies (c.cidrs.count > 0
                                           and c.cidrs.all(r => valid_cidr(r))))
    requires: default_lease_duration = null or default_lease_duration > 0.seconds
    requires: max_lease_duration = null or max_lease_duration > 0.seconds
    requires: default_lease_duration = null or default_lease_duration <= config.global_max_lease_duration
    requires: max_lease_duration = null or max_lease_duration <= config.global_max_lease_duration
    requires: max_extension_duration = null or max_extension_duration <= config.global_max_lease_duration
    requires: default_lease_duration = null or max_lease_duration = null
              or default_lease_duration <= max_lease_duration
    requires: allows_extensions implies max_extension_duration > 0.seconds
    -- Same free-to-govern check as create, but a collection already linked to THIS rule
    -- is fine (it is being kept, not stolen).
    requires: collections.all(c => c.access_rule = null
                                    or c.access_rule = rule)
    -- Collections this rule governs today that are no longer wanted; their link is cleared.
    let removed = rule.collections - collections
    ensures: rule.name = name
    ensures: rule.description = description
    ensures: rule.conditions = conditions
    ensures: rule.default_lease_duration = default_lease_duration
    ensures: rule.max_lease_duration = max_lease_duration
    ensures: rule.allows_extensions = allows_extensions
    ensures: rule.max_extension_duration = max_extension_duration
    ensures: rule.single_active_lease = single_active_lease
    ensures: rule.enabled = enabled
    ensures: rule.revision_date = now
    ensures: rule.last_edited_by = admin
    -- Reconcile the links on the collection side: point the desired collections at this
    -- rule and clear it from any it no longer governs.
    ensures:
        for c in collections:
            c.access_rule = rule
        for c in removed:
            c.access_rule = null
    @guidance
        -- Edits bind requests already pinned to this rule: activation and extension
        -- read the rule as it stands (see AccessRequest.rule). Setting enabled = false
        -- makes the pin govern nothing. Approved requests pinned to it then activate
        -- with no condition check, and extensions of leases under it are refused;
        -- its collections become escape paths, so neither grants more than the member
        -- could already reach.
}

rule OrgAdminDeletesAccessRule {
    when: DeleteAccessRule(admin, target_rule)
    requires: CanManageAccessRules(admin, target_rule.organization)
    -- Hard delete: clear the rule's collection links and every request pin to it,
    -- then remove the rule. Each governed collection survives, now ungoverned. A
    -- deleted rule is simply gone — no gating / admin read can find it, so none
    -- needs to filter it out. The name lookup for the rule_deleted audit event is
    -- supplied by the delete command (captured before the delete), so the row need
    -- not survive.
    --
    -- The pin is provenance, not authority (server #8002), so the delete clears it
    -- rather than being blocked by it. A pending or approved request pinned to the
    -- rule then re-resolves against the member's current paths wherever the pin
    -- would have been read (see AccessRequest.rule). Leases already granted live out
    -- their windows. New opens against this rule's collections fall through to the
    -- gating check, which no longer finds this rule and either passes through (if no
    -- other rule attaches) or hits a different rule.
    ensures: target_rule.collections = {}
    ensures:
        for r in AccessRequest where rule = target_rule:
            r.rule = null
    ensures: not exists target_rule
}

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Derived helpers (predicates referenced by rules)
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- `EligibleApprovers(request)`: the users who can Manage request.collection, the
-- collection paired with the governing rule at submit. That is wider than the
-- collection's own assignments, which matters most for a privileged-access product:
--   - a direct or group assignment with Manage on the collection;
--   - an org Owner or Admin, while the org lets admins access all collection items
--     (AllowAdminAccessToAllCollectionItems), with no assignment needed;
--   - a Custom user holding Edit any collection, with no assignment needed.
-- Members of a suspended org still count (the membership is read from the database,
-- not the claims). The pending-request mail goes to the same set minus the requester.
-- The self-approval guard is enforced at the rule level (not in this set).
--
-- The server decides: DecideAccessRequestCommand and CancelAccessRequestCommand
-- authorize through ApproverCollectionAccessQuery. The client's canApprove.ts and
-- ApprovalPrivilegeService only mirror it to hide actions the server would refuse.
deferred EligibleApprovers    -- see: server ApproverCollectionAccessQuery

-- FUTURE, NOT GA: named approvers. Requirements once asked for a rule to name
-- specific approvers (UC2), and the POC editor showed a disabled "Specific people"
-- option. Design narrowed it during PM-39115, where the lead argued a single named
-- approver is a failure point in approval flows, and the shipped human_approval
-- condition carries no settings. PAM-10 (what happens when a named approver leaves
-- the org) depends on the feature and should be closed or re-scoped when the GA cut
-- is made.

-- `CanManageAccessRules(member, organization)`: may author the organization's
-- access rules: an Owner or Admin, or a Custom member holding Manage access rules.
-- Provider users managing the organization are excluded: who may lease credentials
-- out of an organization is not a provider's to change (PM-40211). This is its own
-- authority, separate from approving requests (EligibleApprovers) and from managing
-- rotation connectors. Reading the rules is wider: any confirmed member may list them,
-- conditions included, which is what lets the collection dialog name a collection's
-- rule to a non-admin manager; whether it should stay that wide is PAM-213.
deferred CanManageAccessRules    -- see: server ManageAccessRulesRequirement

-- `EligibleRevokers(lease)`: the operators who may revoke someone's active lease:
-- EligibleApprovers for the lease's request, plus the lease's org admins. Org
-- admins may revoke any single lease directly (from the governance dashboard) —
-- they are not limited to the org-wide kill switch. The holder is not in this set;
-- they end their own lease through MemberEndsOwnLease, which settles to canceled,
-- and so does a revoke whose actor turns out to be the holder.
--
-- Implemented in RevokeAccessLeaseCommand, which serves both: it authorizes the
-- lease's own holder OR anyone who can Manage the lease's collection
-- (CanManageCollectionAsync), and records canceled for the holder and revoked for
-- anyone else. Manage reaches org admins only while the org lets admins access
-- all collection items; whether any Owner or Admin may revoke regardless is
-- PAM-210. Surfaced by the cipher-view banner's "End access" and the My requests
-- tab (holder), and by the approver's History tab and the request dialog (manager).
deferred EligibleRevokers     -- see: server RevokeAccessLeaseCommand; cipher-view-banner ("End access"), approver-inbox

-- `RuleAutomatedConditionsPass(rule, member)`: every non-human condition on `rule`
-- holds for `member`'s current request context (today: the source IP falls inside
-- each ip_allowlist). True when the rule governs nothing (disabled). Asked at two
-- points:
--   - At submit, on a rule WITHOUT human approval only (MemberSubmitsAccessRequest):
--     a failure refuses the submit and saves nothing.
--   - At every activation (ActivateLease), on any rule, asked of the request's pin
--     as the rule stands now; a pin cleared by a delete re-resolves first (see
--     AccessRequest.rule). A failure rejects the activation without consuming the
--     approval.
-- A human-approval rule is therefore checked only at activation: its conditions
-- inform the approver rather than pre-empting them (PM-42256).
--
-- Fail-safe for a stored conditions document the server cannot read (a kind it no
-- longer knows, or malformed JSON): at submit the rule is treated as requiring
-- human approval, so the request goes to a person; at activation the check fails,
-- so the start is refused (PM-42273). Writes never produce such a document; only a
-- legacy or hand-edited row can.
--
-- FIRST GRANTS ONLY. Since PM-42500 an extension is never evaluated against this
-- predicate: it has no submit of its own to refuse, and it never activates (it
-- applies in place).
deferred RuleAutomatedConditionsPass    -- see: server SubmitAccessRequestCommand, ActivateAccessRequestCommand

-- `oldest_rule(candidates)`: selection primitive for governing-rule resolution.
-- Picks the AccessRule with the earliest creation_date from a candidate set,
-- breaking ties on rule id (lowest id wins) so the choice is total and stable.
-- Returns null for an empty set. The candidate set (enabled rules on
-- the collections through which the member reaches the cipher) and the oldest-wins
-- property are computed STRUCTURALLY at the call sites — CipherOpenRequiresAccessRequest
-- and MemberSubmitsAccessRequest — where a requires clause asserts no candidate is
-- older via creation_date. This selector exists only because a where-query yields
-- an unordered Set with no positional `.first`, and to carry the id tie-break,
-- which the spec language has no total-order primitive to express.
--
-- WHY oldest-wins (do not reintroduce the old behaviour): resolution favours
-- neither an automatic grant over one needing human approval nor least-restriction
-- of any kind — the oldest rule's conditions decide outright. Semantic consequence:
-- a newer auto-approve rule on one path does NOT pre-empt an older human-approval
-- rule on another path (or vice versa). A member may be routed to an approver even
-- though a newer path would have auto-granted, because the older rule governs. This
-- is the intended trade: determinism over least-restriction.
--
-- WHY pin at submit: resolution runs ONCE, at MemberSubmitsAccessRequest, and the
-- resolved rule is pinned on the AccessRequest.rule (see that field). Downstream
-- reads the pinned rule and never re-resolves to a different one while the pin
-- stands, so a rule appearing or disappearing on another path cannot move an
-- in-flight request to a different rule. The pin fixes WHICH rule, not its content:
-- later edits to the pinned rule do apply at activation and extension. The
-- oldest-wins property is therefore asserted at the creation transition only.
--
-- This is distinct from — and does not change — the union/OR policy that decides
-- WHETHER the cipher is gated at all (an ungated path bypasses gating; see
-- CipherOpenPassthrough / RuleBypassableCiphers) or the union/OR logic for
-- single_active_lease (see SingleActiveLeaseApplies / RuleAllowsLease).
--
-- The server-side resolver still owns the implementation.
deferred oldest_rule    -- see: server-side GoverningRuleResolver

-- `RuleBypassableCiphers(rule)`: the ciphers in this rule's collections that a
-- member could also reach through some collection with NO enabled access rule —
-- so the rule's gating does not actually protect them (see the cipher-open
-- gating logic). Empty when the rule's collections share no ciphers with ungated
-- collections. Computed server-side (it must scan ciphers and their collection
-- membership).
--
-- This is the PER-PATH bypass. The org-wide export permission is a separate,
-- per-caller one that no rule-level warning captures — see
-- VaultExportBypassesLeasing.
-- IMPLEMENTED with a deliberate narrowing: the server answers the ungated COLLECTIONS, not the
-- ciphers. Naming a cipher requires decrypting it from the caller's own vault, and an admin outside
-- the collection -- the admin being warned -- has none of those ciphers there, so the cipher list
-- would be empty for exactly the person who needs it. The collections are reliably nameable by any
-- admin and are what remediation acts on. A non-empty list is the warning condition.
--
-- NOT SURFACED: the server computes it, but no surface exposes it. The admin-console warning it
-- drove was removed because the predicate is collection-level and not member-aware, so it warned
-- about ungated collections nobody is assigned to. Re-exposing it needs the member expansion
-- first.
deferred RuleBypassableCiphers    -- see: server-side ListRuleBypassableCiphersQuery

-- NAMED BYPASS: re-filing under a lease. The third deliberate bypass, and the only
-- one a MEMBER can make. MemberChangesGatedCiphers lets a lease holder who may edit a
-- gated item change which collections it is in, including adding it to a collection
-- with no rule. The review kept this: a lease holder may re-file what they hold. From
-- then on the item has an ungated path, so it is ungated for them and for everyone in
-- that collection, with no lease, no approver and no PAM audit event (the org event
-- log records only Cipher_UpdatedCollections). RuleBypassableCiphers then counts the
-- new path like any other.
--
-- It interacts with RotateOnAccessEnd. Rotation exists so that a copy taken during a
-- lease goes stale when the lease ends; a re-filed item keeps the member attached to
-- every future rotated value, so a time-boxed grant becomes standing access that
-- outlives rotation. Whether the move should warn or be audited is PAM-203. The same
-- re-file through the admin endpoint, by a Custom user with Edit any collection and
-- no lease, is PAM-202.

-- `GatedFor(member, cipher)`: the member reaches the cipher, and only through
-- collections that carry an enabled AccessRule. It is the has_ungated_path test the
-- CipherOpen* rules spell out, negated, named here so the write and export rules can
-- reuse it.
deferred GatedFor    -- see: server CipherLeaseGate / GoverningRuleResolver

-- `PartialView(member, cipher)`: the GatedCipherView the server returns for `cipher`
-- to `member`, built from the PartialCipherData allowlist plus the member's own flags.
-- Every gated outcome carries it in place of the cipher.
deferred PartialView    -- see: server Partial*CipherResponseModel

-- `CanExportOrganizationVault(member, organization)`: whether the member may run the
-- whole-vault export: Owner, Admin, or Custom holding Import/export
-- (accessImportExport). Owned by the organization permission model, not by PAM; PAM
-- only reads it. Holding it overrides leasing outright (VaultExportBypassesLeasing): it
-- is not an AccessRule condition, no rule can narrow it, and no lease, request or
-- approval stands between the holder and a gated credential.
deferred CanExportOrganizationVault    -- see: server VaultExportAuthorizationHandler, OrganizationExportController

-- `ExportedCiphers(member, organization)`: the ciphers a whole-vault export covers, as
-- resolved by the existing export stack. PAM neither computes nor filters it; it is
-- named so VaultExportBypassesLeasing can state that gating subtracts nothing from it.
deferred ExportedCiphers    -- see: vault export stack (outside PAM)

-- `ManagedExportCiphers(member, organization)`: the ciphers in the collections the
-- member manages, which is the scope of a member's own export. Resolved by the export
-- stack; ManagedCollectionsExportExcludesGated subtracts the gated ones.
deferred ManagedExportCiphers    -- see: vault export stack (outside PAM)

-- FUTURE, NOT PLANNED: a pre-submit forecast. The spec once described a per-caller
-- dry run of evaluation (auto_approve / needs_approval / would_deny) shown before the
-- member submits, together with the governing rule's conditions. It was never built:
-- the request form shapes itself from pre_check instead (see RequestAccessModal),
-- whose approval mode is structural and deliberately the same for every caller of a
-- rule (PM-42256). A would_deny answer would also be an unaudited allowlist probe,
-- which the server's deliberately vague refusal text exists to prevent. Whether to
-- bring the forecast back is PAM-209.

-- `SingleActiveLeaseApplies(member, cipher)`: true when EVERY collection through
-- which `member` reaches `cipher` is governed by an enabled AccessRule whose
-- single_active_lease is set. By the union/OR rule (the same logic that lets an
-- ungated path bypass gating), the singleton binds only when no path offers an
-- escape — no path is ungated and none is governed by a rule without
-- single_active_lease. Evaluated independently of which rule governs the request
-- decision: it is a property of the member's whole set of paths to the cipher, not
-- the single resolved gating rule.
deferred SingleActiveLeaseApplies    -- see: server-side lease start / rule evaluation

-- `RuleAllowsLease(member, cipher)`: gate on single_active_lease, honored by union.
-- When SingleActiveLeaseApplies(member, cipher) holds (all of the member's paths to
-- the cipher are single_active_lease-governed) the singleton binds: returns false if
-- any other lease is already active for this cipher (across all users), so a second
-- cannot start. When it does not hold — the member has at least one ungated or
-- non-single_active_lease path — the member is unconstrained and this is always
-- true. Checked at lease start (ActivateLease) against the
-- member's current paths to the request's cipher, not at approval, since that is when
-- the lease comes into being.
--
-- Contention policy: when the cipher's slot is taken the start is rejected and the
-- approved request stays `approved` — the member retries when it frees, bounded by
-- the activation window. No server-side queue; the member polls, with availability
-- surfaced by pre_check (can_start_lease / slot_frees_at, PM-42446) in
-- RequestAccessModal and by the cipher-view banner.
deferred RuleAllowsLease    -- see: server SingleActiveLeaseEvaluator

-- `HoldsPamSeat(member, organization)`: the member may use PAM in that
-- organization. All three must hold: the member has a PAM seat (AccessPam), the
-- organization's plan includes PAM (UsePam), and the organization is enabled (not
-- suspended). Provider users acting in a client organization never hold one.
-- Read from the member's token claims, so a change takes effect at their next token
-- refresh (PM-39423).
--
-- Required to ACQUIRE access: submitting a request, activating a lease, extending
-- one, and having a held lease release the cipher (CipherOpenWithActiveLease).
-- Deliberately NOT required to give access back or to look: withdrawing a request,
-- ending or revoking a lease, and every read stay open, so a de-licensed member can
-- still return what they hold and the client can explain the block. A lease held
-- when the seat goes stays active in every read but releases nothing
-- (CipherOpenBlockedUnlicensed). There is no feature flag of its own; it rides on
-- the PAM flag.
deferred HoldsPamSeat    -- see: server PamLicenseGuard, CipherLeaseGate.LeaseCanRelease

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Actors
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

actor Member {
    identified_by: User
}

actor Approver {
    identified_by: User
    within: Organization
    -- Approvers are surfaced per-request via EligibleApprovers.
}

actor OrgAdmin {
    identified_by: User
    within: Organization
    -- Granted by the existing admin-console permission model.
}

actor RuleAuthor {
    identified_by: User
    within: Organization
    -- An Owner or Admin, or a Custom member holding Manage access rules; never a
    -- provider user (CanManageAccessRules).
}

actor CollectionManager {
    identified_by: User
    within: Collection
    -- Anyone who can edit the collection, which is not the same as authoring rules.
}

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces (boundaries of the PAM client UI)
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

surface CipherViewBanner {
    -- The requester's entry point on an open gated cipher: renders exactly one of
    -- five states. Unlicensed comes first and replaces every other state, an active
    -- lease included (CipherOpenBlockedUnlicensed): it explains the block and offers
    -- nothing. Otherwise: active lease (countdown, extend, end), approved request
    -- (start, withdraw), pending request (withdraw), or none of those (request
    -- access, which folds out RequestAccessModal inline). Also the surface that reveals the
    -- credential in place the moment a lease becomes active, without reopening the
    -- item. See cipher-view-banner.component, bound to the CIPHER_VIEW_BANNER seam;
    -- the reveal itself goes through the GATED_CIPHER_RELOADER seam.
    --
    -- It also renders on an item the server no longer gates for the caller while they
    -- still hold a live lease on it (the item was since filed into a collection with no
    -- rule), so the holder can still extend or end that lease (PM-43689).
    facing viewer: Member

    context target_cipher: Cipher

    exposes:
        target_cipher.collections
        -- Whether the viewer holds a PAM seat. Read from the viewer's own membership,
        -- with no round trip, so the block shows even when the state read fails.
        HoldsPamSeat(viewer, target_cipher.organization)
        -- Caller's lease/request state for this cipher, surfaced for the badge.
        AccessLease where requester = viewer and cipher = target_cipher and status = active
        AccessRequest where requester = viewer and cipher = target_cipher and status = pending
        -- Approved-but-unactivated request: banner offers "Start access".
        AccessRequest where requester = viewer and cipher = target_cipher
                                               and status = approved and lease = null

    provides:
        OpenCipher(viewer, target_cipher)
        MemberActivatesLease(viewer, request)
            when request.requester = viewer and request.cipher = target_cipher
                                             and request.status = approved
             and request.lease = null
             and HoldsPamSeat(viewer, target_cipher.organization)
        -- Withdraw, on the pending and approved states. Hidden behind the unlicensed
        -- block here, but the vault row's menu (VaultRowAccessState) and the My
        -- requests tab still offer it, since giving access back needs no seat.
        MemberCancelsRequest(viewer, request)
            when request.requester = viewer and request.cipher = target_cipher
             and (request.status = pending
                  or (request.status = approved and request.lease = null))
             and HoldsPamSeat(viewer, target_cipher.organization)
        -- Extending and ending the lease are offered here as well, through
        -- extend-lease-dialog.component; ActiveLeasesPage states them once for every
        -- screen that offers them.
}

surface VaultRowAccessState {
    -- A vault row for an item gated for the viewer (a partial row). Presentation over
    -- reads modelled elsewhere, apart from one write: the row menu's withdraw. See
    -- vault-row-lease-badge/ and the VaultRowAccessActionsService seam.
    facing viewer: Member

    context target_cipher: Cipher

    exposes:
        -- The row badge: the viewer's own state on the item, which the SDK ranks
        -- into one badge (pending, ready to start, or active with a countdown).
        AccessLease where requester = viewer and cipher = target_cipher and status = active
        AccessRequest where requester = viewer and cipher = target_cipher and in_flight

    provides:
        -- Withdraw the viewer's outstanding request from the row menu, with the
        -- banner's semantics: pending, or approved and never started.
        MemberCancelsRequest(viewer, request)
            when request.requester = viewer and request.cipher = target_cipher
             and (request.status = pending
                  or (request.status = approved and request.lease = null))

    @guidance
        -- Filters: the sidebar's "Controlled access" group narrows the item list by
        -- the same badge. "Privileged" keeps every item gated for the viewer, and
        -- "My requests" keeps those with a pending, ready or active badge.
        --
        -- Collection level: the collection row's "Privileged" pill, the sidebar lock
        -- and the banner above a governed collection's item list describe the
        -- COLLECTION (it carries an enabled rule), not every item in it. An item the
        -- viewer also reaches through a collection with no rule opens normally
        -- (CipherOpenPassthrough) and renders as an ordinary row.
}

surface PamNavBadge {
    -- The count beside the Access requests nav item: the viewer's own requests that
    -- still need them, unioned by request id with the pending requests awaiting the
    -- viewer's decision. See pam-nav-badge.service.
    facing viewer: Member

    exposes:
        for r in AccessRequest where requester = viewer and actionable_for_requester:
            r.id
        for r in AccessRequest where status = pending
                                  and now < lease_not_after
                                  and viewer in EligibleApprovers(r):
            r.id
}

surface RequestAccessModal {
    -- Shown when the member opens a gated cipher they hold no lease/request for
    -- (CipherOpenNeedsRequest). No AccessRequest exists yet: the member optionally
    -- picks a scheduled window (otherwise the request is on-demand and the window
    -- defaults from the rule's duration at submit), adds a reason, and submits,
    -- which creates it (MemberSubmitsAccessRequest). Dismissing without submitting
    -- creates nothing.
    --
    -- Implemented as a fold-out INSIDE CipherViewBanner rather than a separate modal,
    -- so the requester never loses sight of the item they are asking for. Which fields
    -- it collects comes from pre_check: a duration alone on the automatic path, a
    -- window plus a required reason on the human path. The name is kept because the
    -- copy keys (requestAccessModal*) are shared with it.
    facing requester: Member

    context target_cipher: Cipher

    -- Same gating test and governing-rule resolution as MemberSubmitsAccessRequest,
    -- so pre_check describes the rule that would be pinned on submit (oldest wins;
    -- ties on rule id via oldest_rule). Advisory: a rule added/removed on another
    -- path before submit could change the pinned rule.
    let member_memberships = CollectionMembership where member = requester
                                                    and collection in target_cipher.collections
    let has_ungated_path = member_memberships.any(mm =>
        (AccessRule where enabled
                       and mm.collection in collections).count = 0)
    let member_collections = member_memberships -> collection
    let governing_candidates = AccessRule where enabled
                                            and collections.any(c => c in member_collections)
    let gating_rule = oldest_rule(governing_candidates)

    -- Exactly what pre_check returns, and nothing about the rule beyond it: the
    -- form never shows the rule's conditions.
    exposes:
        target_cipher.collections
        -- The approval mode: whether the governing rule carries human_approval.
        -- Structural, so every caller of a rule gets the same answer whatever their
        -- IP or clock (PM-42256). A cipher that is not gated reports automatic.
        gating_rule.has_human_approval when not has_ungated_path
        -- The duration the form pre-selects, and the longest duration or window
        -- span the server will accept.
        gating_rule.default_lease_duration when not has_ungated_path
        gating_rule.max_lease_duration when not has_ungated_path
        -- Whether a lease could actually be started right now — i.e. the per-cipher
        -- single_active_lease slot is currently free for this member. Only binds when
        -- all of the member's paths to the cipher are single_active_lease-governed
        -- (union/OR); otherwise always startable. Lets the modal warn that an
        -- otherwise-auto_approve request could not be started yet because another lease
        -- is active on this cipher. A current-state hint, re-checked for real at start.
        --
        -- Surfaced with the time the slot frees (the blocking lease's not_after, latest
        -- one wins), so the requester gets a retry time rather than polling. Deliberately
        -- WITHOUT the holder's identity: same-collection members can already enumerate
        -- each other, but who is using which privileged credential right now is
        -- behavioural data only approvers see (ActiveLeasesPage). Revisit only behind an
        -- org opt-in.
        RuleAllowsLease(requester, target_cipher)

    provides:
        MemberSubmitsRequest(requester, target_cipher, reason, window_start, window_end)
}

surface MyAccessRequestsPage {
    -- "My requests": the caller's own AccessRequest rows, bounded by the shared
    -- history window. Implemented as two tabs of the Access requests page rather than
    -- one list — my-requests-tab.component holds what is still actionable (pending
    -- requests and active leases) and history-tab.component holds what has resolved —
    -- because the two carry different actions. Each row links to the single-request
    -- view (RequestDetailView).
    facing viewer: Member

    context viewer: Member

    exposes:
        for r in AccessRequest where requester = viewer
                                  and (status = pending
                                       or resolved_at >= now - config.history_window):
            r.id
            r.cipher
            r.status
            r.submitted_at
            r.resolved_at
            r.approver_comment
            r.lease_not_before
            r.lease_not_after

        -- Activation countdown for approved-but-unactivated requests.
        for r in AccessRequest where requester = viewer and status = approved
                                  and lease = null and extension_of = null:
            r.actionable_until

        -- The outstanding rows of the My requests tab: what still needs the viewer
        -- (ActionableForRequester), the same set PamNavBadge counts.
        for r in AccessRequest where requester = viewer and actionable_for_requester:
            r.id

    provides:
        -- Withdraw a not-yet-activated request, pending or approved, while its
        -- window is open. Past lease_not_after the request reads as expired and
        -- MemberCancelsOwnRequest refuses it, so Cancel is withheld. The window end is
        -- exclusive, as everywhere.
        MemberCancelsRequest(viewer, request)
            when request.requester = viewer
             and request.extension_of = null
             and (request.status = pending
                  or (request.status = approved and request.lease = null))
             and now < request.lease_not_after
        -- Activate an approved request: start the lease when ready. Offered only
        -- while the window is open; past lease_not_after the server rejects
        -- activation, so the action is withheld.
        MemberActivatesLease(viewer, request)
            when request.status = approved and request.requester = viewer
             and request.lease = null
             and request.extension_of = null
             and now < request.lease_not_after
}

surface ActiveLeasesPage {
    -- Caller's currently-active leases, each of which they may end early
    -- (canceled, MemberEndsOwnLease) or extend once.
    --
    -- No page of its own. Both actions it provides ARE reachable: the
    -- "Active access" section of my-requests-tab.component ends a lease, and
    -- cipher-view-banner.component ends or extends the lease on the item itself
    -- (through extend-lease-dialog.component). This surface is kept as the domain
    -- statement of what a lease holder may do to their own lease, independently of
    -- which screen offers it.
    facing viewer: Member

    context viewer: Member

    exposes:
        for l in AccessLease where requester = viewer and status = active:
            l.cipher
            l.collection
            l.not_before
            l.not_after

    provides:
        MemberEndsOwnLease(viewer, lease, reason)
            when lease.requester = viewer and lease.status = active
        -- Offered only on a live lease that has not been extended yet, under a rule
        -- that allows it. Only the API reaches a lease that has already ended (see
        -- ExtensionDeniedParentGone). The dialog should offer only durations within
        -- the rule's cap; today it offers presets above it, which the server refuses
        -- (PAM-228).
        MemberRequestsExtension(viewer, lease, duration, reason)
            when lease.requester = viewer and lease.status = active
             and lease.rule.allows_extensions
             and (AccessRequest where extension_of = lease).count = 0
}

surface ApproverInbox {
    -- The approver's queue of pending requests plus a history view of resolved
    -- items. Implemented as the Approvals tab of the Access requests page
    -- (approvals-tab.component) plus the approver scope of its History tab, rather
    -- than a page of its own; the tab is hidden entirely from a member with no
    -- approval privileges.
    --
    -- Its two reads and its decision write go through the SDK's approver-scoped
    -- ApprovalsClient (list_inbox, list_history, decide), like every other PAM call.
    -- Revoke and cancel-approval are served by LeasesClient::end and
    -- AccessRequestsClient::cancel.
    facing viewer: Approver

    context viewer: Approver

    exposes:
        -- Pending bucket: requests the approver may decide. Extensions never appear
        -- here — since PM-42500 they are approved at creation and never rest at
        -- `pending` (see AutoApproveExtension). An approver sees a member's first
        -- grant and nothing after it.
        for r in AccessRequest where status = pending
                                  and viewer in EligibleApprovers(r):
            r.id
            r.cipher
            r.collection
            r.requester
            r.lease_not_before
            r.lease_not_after
            r.reason
            r.submitted_at

        -- History bucket: requests the approver has decided or that resolved
        -- against this approver's scope, bounded by the shared history window.
        for r in AccessRequest where status != pending
                                  and viewer in EligibleApprovers(r)
                                  and resolved_at >= now - config.history_window:
            r.id
            r.cipher
            r.collection
            r.requester
            r.status
            r.resolved_at
            r.approver_comment
            r.lease

    provides:
        ApproverDecides(viewer, request, verdict, comment)
            when request.status = pending and viewer != request.requester
        -- Withdraw an approval that was never started (→ denied), offered on the
        -- History tab since the request is no longer pending. Only while the window
        -- is open; past lease_not_after the request reads as expired and
        -- ApproverRetractsRequest refuses it. A pending request is decided instead,
        -- and an extension is never withdrawn.
        ApproverCancelsRequest(viewer, request)
            when viewer != request.requester
             and request.status = approved
             and request.lease = null
             and request.extension_of = null
             and now < request.lease_not_after
        ApproverRevokesLeaseAction(viewer, lease, reason)
            when lease.status = active
}

surface RequestDetailView {
    -- The single-request view at /pam/requests/:id: a dialog over the Access
    -- requests tabs, reached from any request row and from every PAM mail
    -- (approvers are mailed this link when a request needs their decision). One view
    -- serves both sides, and its actions follow who is viewing: the requester gets
    -- theirs, an approver who can Manage the request's collection gets theirs, and
    -- neither is shown the other side's (PAM-150). An approver landing cold from mail
    -- gets the Approvals tab behind the dialog. The viewer must authenticate and
    -- unlock before reaching the route (PAM-50). See access-request-route.component
    -- and access-request-dialog.component.
    facing viewer: Member

    context request: AccessRequest

    exposes:
        request.cipher
        request.collection
        request.requester
        request.status
        request.reason
        request.lease_not_before
        request.lease_not_after
        request.submitted_at
        request.resolved_at
        request.approver
        request.approver_comment
        request.lease

    provides:
        -- The requester's actions, under MyAccessRequestsPage's guards.
        MemberCancelsRequest(viewer, request)
            when request.requester = viewer
             and request.extension_of = null
             and (request.status = pending
                  or (request.status = approved and request.lease = null))
             and now < request.lease_not_after
        MemberActivatesLease(viewer, request)
            when request.requester = viewer
             and request.status = approved
             and request.lease = null
             and request.extension_of = null
             and now < request.lease_not_after
        -- An approver's actions, under ApproverInbox's guards.
        ApproverDecides(viewer, request, verdict, comment)
            when viewer in EligibleApprovers(request)
             and viewer != request.requester
             and request.status = pending
        ApproverCancelsRequest(viewer, request)
            when viewer in EligibleApprovers(request)
             and viewer != request.requester
             and request.status = approved
             and request.lease = null
             and request.extension_of = null
             and now < request.lease_not_after
        -- Ending the lease the request produced: the requester ends their own
        -- access, a managing approver revokes it.
        MemberEndsOwnLease(viewer, request.lease, reason)
            when request.requester = viewer
             and request.lease != null
             and request.lease.status = active
        ApproverRevokesLeaseAction(viewer, request.lease, reason)
            when viewer in EligibleApprovers(request)
             and viewer != request.requester
             and request.lease != null
             and request.lease.status = active
}

surface AccessRulesAdminPage {
    -- CRUD page for an org's access rules, for rule authors only (the route guard
    -- checks canManageAccessRules). See access-rules.component.
    facing admin: RuleAuthor

    context org: Organization

    exposes:
        for rule in AccessRule where organization = org:
            rule.name
            rule.description
            rule.conditions
            rule.collections
            rule.default_lease_duration
            rule.max_lease_duration
            rule.allows_extensions
            rule.max_extension_duration
            rule.single_active_lease
            rule.enabled
            rule.revision_date

    provides:
        CreateAccessRule(admin, org, name, description, conditions,
                         collections, default_lease_duration, max_lease_duration,
                         allows_extensions, max_extension_duration,
                         single_active_lease, enabled)
        UpdateAccessRule(admin, rule, name, description, conditions,
                         collections, default_lease_duration, max_lease_duration,
                         allows_extensions, max_extension_duration,
                         single_active_lease, enabled)
        DeleteAccessRule(admin, rule)

    @guidance
        -- The editor offers preset durations only; the rules themselves accept any
        -- value within the global ceiling, so a rule written through the API can
        -- hold one the presets do not. Editing such a rule in the UI snaps the
        -- value to the nearest preset today (PAM-227). Who last edited a rule is
        -- not shown here; the audit trail records it (PM-39117 shipped without it).
}

surface CollectionAccessRuleCallout {
    -- Shown inside the collection edit dialog, naming the access rules that gate the
    -- collection's items so an admin editing WHO can reach a collection can see that a
    -- rule already governs WHEN. Read-only and informational: it grants nothing and
    -- blocks nothing, so a failed read simply hides it. Only rendered while editing an
    -- existing collection — there is nothing to gate before one exists. See
    -- collection-access-rule-callout.component, bound to the
    -- COLLECTION_ACCESS_RULE_CALLOUT seam.
    --
    -- It faces whoever can edit the collection, rule author or not: a collection
    -- manager who may not change the rule still sees it, through the member-wide
    -- rule read (see CanManageAccessRules).
    facing admin: CollectionManager

    context target_collection: Collection

    exposes:
        -- Every enabled rule governing this collection, with the conditions it
        -- enforces. Plural even though Collection.access_rule is a single reference:
        -- the client reads the org's rules and filters by membership rather than
        -- following the link, so it reports whatever the server actually returns
        -- instead of assuming the cardinality holds.
        for rule in AccessRule where enabled and target_collection in collections:
            rule.id
            rule.name
            rule.conditions
            rule.single_active_lease
}

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

invariant AtMostOneLeasePerActivatedRequest {
    -- Single-use approved request: a request that has a lease has exactly one,
    -- and an extension never has any — it applies by extending the parent in place
    -- and never mints a lease of its own. An approved request with no lease is one
    -- that has not started.
    for request in AccessRequest:
        (AccessLease where request = request).count <= 1
            and (request.extension_of != null
                 implies (AccessLease where request = request).count = 0)
}

invariant NoLeaseWithoutActivatedRequest {
    -- A lease exists only because an approved first-grant request was started, and
    -- that request stays approved for the rest of its life, whatever the lease's
    -- later status: it never lapses, and it cannot be canceled or retracted.
    for lease in AccessLease:
        lease.request.status = approved and lease.request.extension_of = null
}

invariant AtMostOneActiveLeasePerRequesterCipher {
    -- A member holds at most one active lease per cipher. Holds transitively —
    -- MemberSubmitsAccessRequest is blocked while an active lease exists, an
    -- approved request is single-use, and an extension extends the parent in
    -- place rather than minting a lease — but is pinned here as a first-class
    -- obligation because reads rely on it: the `.first` on active_leases in
    -- CipherOpenWithActiveLease is deterministic only because the set is a
    -- singleton.
    for a in AccessLease:
        for b in AccessLease:
            a != b and a.requester = b.requester and a.cipher = b.cipher
                implies not (a.status = active and b.status = active)
}

invariant AtMostOneInFlightRequestPerRequesterCipher {
    -- At most one request in flight per (requester, cipher): a first grant still
    -- pending, or approved and not started (AccessRequest.in_flight).
    -- MemberSubmitsAccessRequest refuses to create a request while another is in
    -- flight, and nothing else creates one. Licenses the `.first` on
    -- approved_requests in CipherOpenWithApprovedRequest. A started request stays
    -- approved but is no longer in flight, so a member can hold many approved
    -- requests for one cipher over time. Extensions are never in flight: they are
    -- created already decided, and bounded separately, at most one per lease
    -- (AtMostOneExtensionPerLease). The server check is not yet race-safe against
    -- two submits at once (PAM-223, PAM-215).
    for a in AccessRequest:
        for b in AccessRequest:
            a != b and a.requester = b.requester and a.cipher = b.cipher
                implies not (a.in_flight and b.in_flight)
}

invariant AtMostOneExtensionPerLease {
    -- A lease is extended at most once (PM-40526), whatever became of the attempt:
    -- a denied late extension counts too. So a lease's whole life is at most its
    -- window plus one max_extension_duration; after that a fresh request runs the
    -- full evaluation, human approval included. The server re-counts under a
    -- per-lease lock, so two concurrent attempts cannot both land.
    for a in AccessRequest where extension_of != null:
        for b in AccessRequest where extension_of != null:
            a != b implies a.extension_of != b.extension_of
}

invariant SelfApprovalForbidden {
    -- Enforced by the server's DecideAccessRequestCommand (and, for a retraction,
    -- CancelAccessRequestCommand); the client's canApprove.ts only mirrors it.
    for request in AccessRequest where status in {approved, denied}
                                    and approver != null:
        request.approver != request.requester
}

invariant SingleActiveLeaseRespected {
    -- Per-cipher singleton, honored by union. Two distinct leases for the same
    -- cipher cannot both be active when the singleton binds for both requesters (every
    -- path each requester has to the cipher is single_active_lease-governed). A requester
    -- with an ungated or non-single_active_lease path is unconstrained, so such a
    -- lease may legitimately coexist — a deliberate bypass, like RuleBypassableCiphers.
    for a in AccessLease:
        for b in AccessLease:
            a != b
                and a.cipher = b.cipher
                and a.status = active
                and b.status = active
                and SingleActiveLeaseApplies(a.requester, a.cipher)
            implies not SingleActiveLeaseApplies(b.requester, b.cipher)
}

-- One live rule per collection is now structural, not an invariant: the link lives on
-- Collection.access_rule as a single reference, so a collection cannot hold two. See the
-- Collection entity and AccessRule.collections.

invariant OrgUniqueRuleNames {
    -- Storage constraint (intentional gap), silent in the rule model but enforced by
    -- the implementation: a live rule's name is unique within its organization
    -- (create and update reject a duplicate, compared case-insensitively — the spec
    -- has no case-fold primitive, so this expresses the distinctness only). Names are
    -- otherwise bounded to 1..256 characters (see OrgAdminCreatesAccessRule).
    for a in AccessRule:
        for b in AccessRule:
            a != b and a.organization = b.organization implies a.name != b.name
}

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- PLANNED, NOT BUILT
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Everything in this section is GA backlog with no server, SDK or client
-- implementation behind it. It is kept, not cut: the tickets below are open, though
-- whether each makes GA waits on the MUST-versus-fast-follow cut (PAM-67). Nothing
-- outside this section depends on it being built, except the leasing-freeze guard on
-- ActivateLease, which is marked there as not enforced today.
--
-- Org-wide kill switch and leasing freeze: epic PAM-15 (PAM-16 force-release,
-- PAM-17 kill switch with an optional freeze, PAM-18 freeze indicator and lift), and
-- the member-side stories PAM-46 and PAM-49. Their audit kinds are reserved but never
-- written (pam-audit.allium). When the design spike PAM-140 is groomed, reconcile
-- three points: whether a freeze also blocks submitting a request (PAM-15's criteria)
-- or only starting one (this spec); the audit and notification outcomes the kill
-- switch and the unblock should emit; and the user-facing name ("Turn off all
-- access", PAM-141).
--
-- Governance dashboard: epic PAM-115, story PAM-85, design in PAM-140. The server
-- already serves the scope-wide lease lists (GET /leases/active and
-- /leases/history, scoped to the collections the caller manages); the SDK wraps
-- list_active only, and there is no summary read and no client. The surface below
-- is the POC's design, to be re-derived from PAM-140 once it is groomed, audience
-- included.

-- An org-wide block on starting new leases, engaged by an admin (typically
-- alongside the kill switch). While one exists for an organization, no approved
-- request can be activated into a lease there. Its existence IS the "blocked" state;
-- removing it lifts the block. At most one per organization.
entity LeasingFreeze {
    organization: Organization
    engaged_at: Timestamp
    engaged_by: User
}

rule OrgAdminTriggersKillSwitch {
    -- Org-wide kill switch revokes all active leases in the organization in a
    -- single bulk operation. Optionally also blocks new leases from starting
    -- (engages a LeasingFreeze) so the org stays locked down until an admin
    -- explicitly unblocks. Planned for the governance dashboard. Emits no audit or
    -- notification outcome yet; PAM-17/18 and PAM-140 require both.
    when: OrgKillSwitchActivated(admin, organization, block_new_leases)
    ensures:
        for lease in AccessLease where collection.organization = organization and status = active:
            lease.status = revoked
            lease.ended_at = now
            lease.ended_by = admin
        if block_new_leases and not exists LeasingFreeze{organization: organization}:
            LeasingFreeze.created(
                organization: organization,
                engaged_at: now,
                engaged_by: admin
            )
}

rule OrgAdminUnblocksNewLeases {
    -- Lifts an org-wide leasing freeze so approved requests can be activated again.
    when: OrgUnblocksNewLeases(admin, organization)
    let freeze = LeasingFreeze{organization: organization}
    requires: exists freeze
    ensures: not exists freeze
}

invariant AtMostOneLeasingFreezePerOrg {
    for a in LeasingFreeze:
        for b in LeasingFreeze:
            a != b implies a.organization != b.organization
}

surface GovernanceDashboard {
    -- The POC's design: a per-collection summary table for org admins (rule,
    -- pending counts, active counts, last activity) and the scope-wide active and
    -- recently-ended lease lists. The lease lists are the part the server already
    -- serves (GET /leases/active, /leases/history).
    facing admin: OrgAdmin

    context org: Organization

    exposes:
        -- Strip totals.
        AccessRule where organization = org
                     and collections.count > 0
        AccessRequest where collection.organization = org and status = pending
        -- All active leases in scope, surfaced by GET /leases/active.
        AccessLease where collection.organization = org and status = active
        -- All recently-ended leases (expired, revoked or canceled) in scope, within the
        -- shared history window — surfaced by GET /leases/history. A lease's end is its
        -- ended_at (revoked or canceled) or its not_after (expired).
        AccessLease where collection.organization = org
                     and ((status in {revoked, canceled} and ended_at >= now - config.history_window)
                          or (status = expired and not_after >= now - config.history_window))

        -- Per-collection rows.
        for col in Collection where organization = org:
            col.name
            AccessRule where col in collections
            AccessRequest where collection = col and status = pending
            AccessLease where collection = col and status = active

    provides:
        -- Admins may revoke any single active lease directly, without the
        -- org-wide kill switch.
        ApproverRevokesLeaseAction(admin, lease, reason)
            when lease.collection.organization = org and lease.status = active
}

surface KillSwitchControl {
    -- The org-wide kill switch control, planned for the governance dashboard.
    facing admin: OrgAdmin

    context org: Organization

    exposes:
        AccessLease where collection.organization = org and status = active
        -- Present while new leases are blocked org-wide.
        LeasingFreeze where organization = org

    provides:
        -- Revoke all active leases; optionally also block new ones from starting.
        OrgKillSwitchActivated(admin, org, block_new_leases)
        -- Lift the block so approved requests can be activated again.
        OrgUnblocksNewLeases(admin, org)
            when exists LeasingFreeze{organization: org}
}

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open questions
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

open question "Should a disabled pinned rule behave like a deleted one (PAM-211)? Today they differ (see AccessRequest.rule). A deleted rule's pin is cleared and re-resolves at activation, so a younger rule on another path can take over. A disabled rule's pin survives and governs nothing, so its approved requests activate with no condition check ('instant access') even when a younger enabled rule on another path would have checked them, and extensions under it are refused. The reviewer's lean: a disabled pin should re-resolve at activation too. The answer decides the formal projection of the rule a pin governs by, which this spec leaves in prose until then."

open question "When a rule's automated conditions refuse a submit, should a visible denied request be saved as well (PAM-206), as PM-42632 chose for late extensions, or is an audit event enough? Today nothing is saved and nothing is audited; PAM-229 adds the audit event. A refused out-of-network attempt that leaves no trace is a detection gap in a privileged-access product, which activation (LeaseActivationRejected) does not have."

open question "Should the server audit a whole-vault export (PAM-204)? It is the only export that carries gated credentials in full (a member's own export leaves them out): a bulk, lease-free release of exactly what the trail exists to account for. Nothing in PAM records it (pam-audit's CredentialAccessed is itself deferred). The only trace is the organization event log's Organization_ClientExportedVault, which the CLIENT sends, so a modified client can skip it, and which never names the ciphers exported. Undecided: a server-written PAM event per export (or per gated cipher in it), or leave export accounting to the org event log."

open question "One extension per lease, or several within a total (PAM-212)? Today a lease extends at most once (AtMostOneExtensionPerLease, since PM-40526), so its whole life is bounded by its window plus one max_extension_duration, and a denied late attempt uses up that one extension. The alternative is any number of extensions while the total stays within the rule's maximum access time. A narrower worry survives either way: on a human-approval rule, is one unapproved increment of up to max_extension_duration (which may be as long as the global ceiling) too much?"

open question "Should lowering a rule's max_lease_duration reach requests and leases already made? Today the cap is checked once, at submit, against the rule as it was then; activation mints the window's end unchanged. So an admin who shortens a rule's maximum does not shorten an approved request that has not started yet, nor a lease that is already running. Should either be cut to the new maximum?"

open question "What should an ip_allowlist restrict (PAM-205)? Today it bounds ACQUISITION, never USE. It is checked when a member submits on a rule without human approval and whenever they start a lease (PM-42273), but no read re-checks the caller's network: CipherLeaseGate releases on a live lease alone, so any lease, extended or not, is readable from anywhere for its whole window. Extensions (PM-42500) are not checked either, which lengthens that unchecked window by at most one max_extension_duration. An admin reading 'only from these CIDRs' may expect more. Options: re-check the allowlist on every lease-backed read of the credential; check the governing rule's automated conditions on the single-item release read only, so an off-allowlist read gets the partial item and the lease is untouched; bind a lease to the client instance that started it; enforce more strictly on some rules only; or keep today's behaviour and say at the admin surface that an allowlist bounds where access starts."

open question "Should the requester be mailed when an approver withdraws their approval? Today a retraction (ApproverRetractsRequest) is pushed but not mailed, so a requester who is not looking at PAM learns of it only when Start is gone. PAM-29, the next change to retraction, would also require the approver to give a reason, which a mail could carry."

open question "Should a submit the automated conditions refused notify anyone beyond the member, who sees the refusal at once? PAM-30 asks for a notification when policy auto-denies a request; since the refusal saves nothing (see the PAM-206 question), there is no request for approvers to see either."

open question "Should expiry be announced? Nothing is sent when a pending request lapses undecided (the approvers who never acted are not told), when an approved request lapses unused (the requester is not told), or when a lease reaches its end. Expiry is derived when read, so any notice would need a sweep or a scheduled send."

-- RESOLVED — what bounds a lease's cumulative life once extensions need no
-- approval (PM-42500). Recorded because the reasoning matters, not just the outcome.
--
-- Before PM-42500 every extension ran the full evaluation, so on a human-approval
-- rule each increment was a fresh human authorization, and that was the stated
-- control on cumulative life. PM-42500 removed approval from extensions, and the
-- spec briefly concluded that nothing bounded a lease any more and a member could
-- self-renew indefinitely. That premise was false when it was written: the server
-- has allowed ONE extension per lease since PM-40526, the same day the spec was
-- first written, and every later extension change kept it. So the bound is
-- arithmetic: one window, capped at submit by max_lease_duration, plus one
-- extension, capped by max_extension_duration, both within the global ceiling.
-- After that the member makes a fresh request, which runs the full evaluation,
-- human approval included. Whether one is the right number is PAM-212.

-- RESOLVED — org suspension. Governance keeps working while an organization is
-- suspended; members' leases stop releasing.
--   - Governance: the collection-scoped reads and actions (approver inbox, lease
--     lists, request details, decide, cancel, revoke) resolve manageable collections
--     partly from the admin's confirmed memberships in the database, which include
--     disabled organizations, so they keep working. The audit trail is org-scoped and
--     authorized by the event-log permission from claims; it stays available because
--     the claims issued for a member include disabled organizations too.
--   - Members: suspension clears PAM entitlement (HoldsPamSeat needs the org
--     enabled), so nobody can submit, activate or extend, and every held lease stops
--     releasing its cipher until the organization is re-enabled. That is the safe
--     side for a suspended organization.

-- RESOLVED — when a member reaches a cipher through multiple gated collections
-- carrying different enabled rules, WHICH rule governs was previously undefined /
-- fragile (an automatic-favoured union/OR pick). Locked down: the OLDEST rule wins
-- — the enabled, non-deleted rule with the earliest creation_date among the
-- member's gated paths to the cipher (ties broken stably on rule id). Deterministic,
-- with no favouring of automatic grants over human approval. Resolution happens
-- once, at request submit time, and the resolved rule is PINNED on the AccessRequest
-- (its RuleId stored at creation). Downstream reads the pinned rule as it stands now
-- and never re-resolves to a different rule while the pin stands; only a rule delete
-- clears the pin (see the AccessRequest.rule field). Trade-off, stated deliberately: a
-- newer auto-approve rule no longer pre-empts an older human-approval rule —
-- determinism over least-restriction. See the oldest_rule selector and the
-- AccessRequest.rule field. NOTE: this is distinct from — and does not change — the
-- union/OR policy that decides WHETHER a cipher is gated at all (one ungated path
-- bypasses gating; see RuleBypassableCiphers) or the union/OR logic for
-- single_active_lease (see SingleActiveLeaseApplies).

-- RESOLVED — deleting a rule that requests are pinned to (server #8002, the same day
-- the spec was first written). The delete clears every pin to the rule before
-- removing it, instead of failing on the request's foreign key or being blocked: the
-- pin is provenance, not authority, and the audit trail already snapshots the rule's
-- name. A request left without a pin re-resolves wherever the pin would have been read
-- (see AccessRequest.rule and OrgAdminDeletesAccessRule).

-- RESOLVED — extending a lease whose rule was deleted. A lease has no rule of its
-- own: its rule is its originating request's pin, which the delete clears. The
-- extension then re-resolves against the member's current paths. When no rule
-- governs, the item no longer needs a lease and the extension is refused ("This item
-- does not require a lease"); when another path's rule governs, that rule's extension
-- settings decide. A pin to a rule that still exists but is disabled refuses the
-- extension (PM-43689). No approver was ever needed to catch this.
